{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-94995a5a0032d16c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2170 paper_2607.23037v1.txt\nSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal\nAttraction in Speed Dating\nYuriko Kikuchi\n\nTakato Hayashi\n\nRyusei Kimura\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nyuriko_kikuchi@jaist.ac.jp\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nhayashi0884@jaist.ac.jp\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nryusei_kimura@jaist.ac.jp\n\nNaoya Inoue\n\nRyo Ishii\n\nShogo Okada\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nnaoya-i@jaist.ac.jp\n\nNTT, Inc.\nYokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan\nryoct.ishii@ntt.com\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nokada-s@jaist.ac.jp\n\narXiv:2607.23037v1 [cs.CL] 25 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract\nLarge language models (LLMs) can predict interpersonal attraction\nfrom conversation transcripts, but it remains unclear what a speech\npredictor can add beyond transcript-only LLM prediction. Using\nJapanese speed-dating conversations, we combine predictions from\na transcript-only LLM and a supervised speech predictor to estimate participants’ reported liking of their partners. We show that\nspeech can complement transcript-only LLM prediction, but that\nthis complementarity is conditional rather than universal. Combining the two predictions significantly improves pairwise ranking\naccuracy over the transcript-only LLM alone in all evaluated conditions. By contrast, gains in per-participant Pearson 𝑟 vary across\nconversation rounds and rating directions, with none significant\nafter correction. Retrospectively, these 𝑟 gains are concentrated\namong participants for whom the speech predictor is more accurate. Speech can therefore retain predictive value even when an\nLLM predicts attraction from transcripts. The relevant question is\nnot simply whether speech helps, but where its complementarity\nemerges.1\n\nCCS Concepts\n• Human-centered computing → Empirical studies in collaborative and social computing; • Applied computing →\nPsychology; • Computing methodologies → Natural language\nprocessing.\n\nKeywords\ninterpersonal attraction; speed dating; large language models; speech\nprocessing; multimodal fusion\n1 Code is available at github.com/yurikomium/speech-llm-complementarity.\n\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercialNoDerivatives 4.0 International License.\nICMI ’26, Napoli, Italy\n© 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2318-6/2026/10\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3776574.3831151\n\nACM Reference Format:\nYuriko Kikuchi, Takato Hayashi, Ryusei Kimura, Naoya Inoue, Ryo Ishii,\nand Shogo Okada. 2026. Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting\nInterpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating. In INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON MULTIMODAL INTERACTION (ICMI ’26), October 05–09, 2026,\nNapoli, Italy. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 9 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/\n3776574.3831151\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nInterpersonal attraction formed during an initial encounter can\nshape whether a relationship continues to develop [5]. Speed dating\nprovides a naturalistic setting for studying how initial attraction\nforms: participants meet multiple potential partners and report\nhow much they like each one. Conversational behavior can be\ninformative in this setting. Pitch convergence, for example, varies\nwith how speakers perceive their partners’ visual attractiveness\nand overall likability [26]. These findings motivate computational\nmodels of attraction from interaction data.\nAutomatic prediction of interpersonal outcomes from conversations has developed along two largely separate lines. One uses\nlarge language models (LLMs) to infer outcomes from transcripts.\nIn a direct study of speed dates, LLMs predicted objective and\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "ab7f9c76-b754-4232-83f9-f84429645d01", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:46:50.765Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "2170 paper_2607.23037v1.txt\nSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal\nAttraction in Speed Dating\nYuriko Kikuchi\n\nTakato Hayashi\n\nRyusei Kimura\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nyuriko_kikuchi@jaist.ac.jp\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nhayashi0884@jaist.ac.jp\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nryusei_kimura@jaist.ac.jp\n\nNaoya Inoue\n\nRyo Ishii\n\nShogo Okada\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nnaoya-i@jaist.ac.jp\n\nNTT, Inc.\nYokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan\nryoct.ishii@ntt.com\n\nJapan Advanced Institute of Science\nand Technology\nNomi, Ishikawa, Japan\nokada-s@jaist.ac.jp\n\narXiv:2607.23037v1 [cs.CL] 25 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract\nLarge language models (LLMs) can predict interpersonal attraction\nfrom conversation transcripts, but it remains unclear what a speech\npredictor can add beyond transcript-only LLM prediction. Using\nJapanese speed-dating conversations, we combine predictions from\na transcript-only LLM and a supervised speech predictor to estimate participants’ reported liking of their partners. We show that\nspeech can complement transcript-only LLM prediction, but that\nthis complementarity is conditional rather than universal. Combining the two predictions significantly improves pairwise ranking\naccuracy over the transcript-only LLM alone in all evaluated conditions. By contrast, gains in per-participant Pearson 𝑟 vary across\nconversation rounds and rating directions, with none significant\nafter correction. Retrospectively, these 𝑟 gains are concentrated\namong participants for whom the speech predictor is more accurate. Speech can therefore retain predictive value even when an\nLLM predicts attraction from transcripts. The relevant question is\nnot simply whether speech helps, but where its complementarity\nemerges.1\n\nCCS Concepts\n• Human-centered computing → Empirical studies in collaborative and social computing; • Applied computing →\nPsychology; • Computing methodologies → Natural language\nprocessing.\n\nKeywords\ninterpersonal attraction; speed dating; large language models; speech\nprocessing; multimodal fusion\n1 Code is available at github.com/yurikomium/speech-llm-complementarity.\n\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercialNoDerivatives 4.0 International License.\nICMI ’26, Napoli, Italy\n© 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2318-6/2026/10\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3776574.3831151\n\nACM Reference Format:\nYuriko Kikuchi, Takato Hayashi, Ryusei Kimura, Naoya Inoue, Ryo Ishii,\nand Shogo Okada. 2026. Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting\nInterpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating. In INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON MULTIMODAL INTERACTION (ICMI ’26), October 05–09, 2026,\nNapoli, Italy. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 9 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/\n3776574.3831151\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nInterpersonal attraction formed during an initial encounter can\nshape whether a relationship continues to develop [5]. Speed dating\nprovides a naturalistic setting for studying how initial attraction\nforms: participants meet multiple potential partners and report\nhow much they like each one. Conversational behavior can be\ninformative in this setting. Pitch convergence, for example, varies\nwith how speakers perceive their partners’ visual attractiveness\nand overall likability [26]. These findings motivate computational\nmodels of attraction from interaction data.\nAutomatic prediction of interpersonal outcomes from conversations has developed along two largely separate lines. One uses\nlarge language models (LLMs) to infer outcomes from transcripts.\nIn a direct study of speed dates, LLMs predicted objective and", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
