{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-84a8860f798d7c04", "type": "tool_result", "content": "100\tIn a direct study of speed dates, LLMs predicted objective and\n101\tsubjective indicators of interaction success from conversation transcripts [24]. The other uses supervised models with lexical, speech,\n102\tdialogue, and visual signals to predict social judgments and outcomes [30, 36]. Together, these lines raise a broad question about\n103\twhich conversational signals remain useful beyond transcript-only\n104\tLLM prediction. We address a narrower question for speed-dating\n105\tattraction: can a supervised speech predictor add predictive value\n106\tbeyond a transcript-only LLM, and where does that value emerge?\n107\tWe study this question using the Multi-Modal Speed Dating corpus [18], which contains Japanese speed-dating conversations with\n108\ttranscripts, speaker-specific audio, and post-conversation liking\n109\tscores. Our task is to predict each participant’s reported liking of\n110\ttheir partners, with an emphasis on differentiation within the participant’s partner set. We define speech-derived complementarity as\n111\tthe additional predictive value contributed by a supervised speech\n112\tpredictor beyond the evaluated transcript-only LLM’s predictions,\n113\twithout claiming that the speech predictor captures information\n114\tfundamentally absent from transcripts.\n115\t\n116\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n117\t\n118\tFor the primary analysis, the transcript-only predictor is Claude\n119\tSonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled (hereafter Claude), evaluated using zero-shot prompting. The supervised speech predictor is built on frozen HuBERT-Large representations (hereafter\n120\tHuBERT). We combine their scalar predictions through weighted\n121\tscore-level late fusion, as shown in Figure 1. This deliberately simple combination serves as a diagnostic for additional predictive\n122\tvalue rather than as a new fusion architecture. We evaluate complementarity across two conversation rounds, two rating directions,\n123\tand participants. Two multimodal LLMs that receive transcripts\n124\tand audio provide auxiliary direct-input comparisons.\n125\tIn summary, we clarify where a supervised speech predictor\n126\tadds predictive value beyond the evaluated transcript-only LLM\n127\tin speed-dating attraction prediction. We do so by examining this\n128\tadded value across evaluation metrics, conversation rounds and\n129\trating directions, and participants, using score-level late fusion only\n130\tas a diagnostic. Supporting analyses assess whether the transcriptonly LLM and speech predictor make redundant predictions by\n131\tquantifying their within-rater prediction overlap and mutual incremental associations. Together, these analyses shift the question\n132\tfrom whether speech helps on average to where its complementarity emerges.\n133\tRelative to Claude, fusion significantly improves pairwise ranking accuracy in all four round-by-rating-direction settings. Gains\n134\tin per-participant Pearson 𝑟 , however, vary across these settings,\n135\tand no paired gain is statistically significant after correction. Retrospectively, participant-level 𝑟 gains are larger where HuBERT is\n136\tmore accurate, although this association is partly expected because\n137\tthe HuBERT prediction enters the fused score. The Claude and\n138\tHuBERT predictions share little within-rater variance, with each\n139\texplaining comparable incremental in-sample variance beyond the\n140\tother. As supporting context, Claude has higher per-participant 𝑟\n141\tthan every tested supervised text model in all four settings.\n142\tTaken together, these findings do not support a blanket claim that\n143\tadding speech uniformly improves attraction prediction. Instead,\n144\tthey show that speech can retain predictive value even when an\n145\tLLM predicts attraction from transcripts. For multimodal social\n146\tprediction, the relevant question is therefore not simply whether\n147\tspeech helps, but where its complementarity emerges.\n148\t\n149\t2 Related Work\n150\t2.1 LLMs for Interpersonal Inference\n151\tBeyond attraction, LLMs have been evaluated as judges of empathic\n152\tcommunication in text-based conversations. When given criteria\n153\tfor each framework and expert-annotated examples, LLM–expert\n154\tagreement approached expert–expert agreement across four evaluative frameworks [21].\n155\tMost directly related to the present paper, Matz et al. [24] applied\n156\tChatGPT to speed-dating transcripts to predict mutual contact exchange as well as subjective outcomes and experiences. ChatGPT’s\n157\tcorrelations with these criteria were modest (𝑟 = .12–.23). For mutual contact exchange specifically, its performance was comparable\n158\tto that of transcript-only human judges (𝑟 = .12 vs. .13). Building on this work, we test whether a supervised speech predictor\n159\tadds predictive value beyond transcript-only LLM predictions for\n160\tcontinuous liking and within-participant partner ranking.\n161\t\n162\tKikuchi et al.\n163\t\n164\t2.2\n165\t\n166\tMultimodal Prediction of Interpersonal\n167\tOutcomes\n168\t\n169\tInterpersonal outcomes have long been studied computationally\n170\tusing verbal and nonverbal features from conversation. Brief observations of expressive behavior can support accurate interpersonal\n171\tjudgments, and nonverbal communication encompasses cues in the\n172\tface, voice, body, touch, and interpersonal space [2, 13]. In speed\n173\tdating, supervised models have used prosodic, dialogue, and lexical\n174\tfeatures to detect flirtation [30] and position, proximity, and motion features from video to predict contact exchange and physical\n175\tattraction [36]. Complementary statistical analyses have linked\n176\tconversational behavior to perceived connection in speed-dating\n177\tencounters [25] and function-word language style matching to mutual romantic interest [17]. Speakers’ pitch convergence also varies\n178\twith their perception of the partner’s visual attractiveness and overall likability [26]. On Japanese speed-dating data, Ishii et al. [18]\n179\tpredicted post-interaction liking scores from information available\n180\tbefore the interaction, including profiles, facial features, and psychometric measures. More recently, frozen self-supervised speech\n181\trepresentations, including HuBERT, have transferred effectively to\n182\tparalinguistic tasks such as emotion recognition [15, 37].\n183\tSantana et al. [33] provide the closest architectural precedent for\n184\tour supervised branches. Their Speech-to-Joy framework trains separate predictors over frozen pretrained text and audio embeddings,\n185\tusing additive attention pooling and modality-specific regression\n186\theads. The framework then averages the two predictors’ output\n187\tscores for enjoyment prediction in human–robot dialogue. We adapt\n188\tthis supervised-branch architecture, but our main comparison pairs\n189\tthe speech predictor with a transcript-only LLM prediction rather\n190\tthan a supervised text prediction.\n191\t\n192\t2.3\n193\t\n194\tLLM Integration in Multimodal Prediction\n195\t\n196\tMultimodal fusion methods are conventionally partitioned into\n197\tfeature-level early fusion and score-level late fusion. Late fusion is\n198\tparticularly well suited when the predictors are trained under different regimes, since each can be optimized independently. Recent\n199\twork has integrated LLMs into multimodal prediction pipelines\n200\tin several ways. Some approaches textualize nonverbal modalities in the prompt [14, 23] or use an LLM for post hoc refinement\n201\tof conventional model outputs [34]. Others use an LLM to integrate modality-specific predictions in natural language rather than\n202\tthrough a weighted score-level combination [8].\n203\tAt the task level, Pereira et al. [28] combined an LLM-derived\n204\tenjoyment score with temporal, visual, and acoustic features in\n205\tsupervised models for human–robot conversations, and separately\n206\tcompared text-only and video-plus-audio LLM inputs. Their supervised target was third-party enjoyment annotations. The LLM\n207\tscore entered the supervised model alongside modality descriptors\n208\trather than being fused at score level with the output of a separately\n209\ttrained modality-specific predictor.\n210\tTwo prior works more specifically combine score-level late fusion with LLMs, but neither treats a transcript-only LLM prediction as an independent branch. In Amin et al. [3], ChatGPT serves\n211\t\n212\t\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n213\t\n214\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n215\t\n216\tFigure 1: Prediction pipeline for F2M (female rating male) and M2F (male rating female). Claude Sonnet 4.6, used with extended\n217\tthinking disabled and zero-shot prompting, predicts liking from the full two-speaker transcript, while frozen HuBERT and a\n218\tsupervised head predict from rater-side speech. Weighted score-level fusion combines the predictions using a fold-selected\n219\tweight 𝑤.\n220\tas an auxiliary text generator whose output is featurized alongside the raw text, so all fused branches take text input. In ZSFuse [20], whose “zero-shot large-scale model plus supervised specialist model” structure parallels ours, the zero-shot branch is itself\n221\taudio-driven rather than transcript-driven. A separate direction\n222\tintegrates audio directly into LLMs: audio-native models such as\n223\tSALMONN [35] integrate pre-trained speech and audio encoders\n224\twith an LLM and let it reason over audio inputs directly. We include\n225\tthis direction as an auxiliary comparison using the MLLM baselines\n226\tGemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audio-mini with text-plus-audio input.\n227\tTogether, these studies leave unresolved whether a separately\n228\ttrained speech predictor improves interpersonal-attraction prediction beyond a transcript-only LLM. We treat a transcript-only\n229\tLLM prediction obtained through zero-shot prompting and a separately trained supervised speech predictor as independent branches,\n230\tcombining their outputs through weighted score-level late fusion.\n231\tWe use this conventional fusion method to diagnose the speech\n232\tpredictor’s additional predictive value beyond the transcript-only\n233\tprediction. Audio-native MLLMs form a separate direct-input route,\n234\twhich we include only as an auxiliary comparison in the present\n235\tstudy.\n236\t\n237\t3 Corpus and Task Definition\n238\t3.1 Speed-Dating Corpus\n239\tWe use the Multi-Modal Speed Dating corpus [18]. It was collected\n240\tin the speed-dating paradigm, a methodologically validated setting\n241\tfor observing initial romantic attraction under controlled yet ecologically valid conditions [12]. The corpus records face-to-face conversations between male-female pairs involving 147 native Japanese\n242\tspeakers: 75 female and 72 male participants. Their ages ranged\n243\tfrom 19 to 60 years, with a mean of 31.9 years and a standard deviation of 8.6 years. All participants had indicated a strong interest\n244\tin meeting future partners.\n245\tParticipants were assigned to age-balanced groups of 5 female\n246\tand 5 male members. Each group held all 25 possible male-female\n247\tpairwise conversations; some participants joined multiple groups.\n248\tThe corpus is organized into two conversation rounds with nominal\n249\tdurations of 5 minutes (Session 1) and 10 minutes (Session 2). Each\n250\tsession contains 624 pairwise conversations for a total of 1,248.\n251\t\n252\tThe same pairs conversed in both rounds, and Session 2 was held\n253\tafter Session 1. Thus, each Session 2 conversation followed a prior\n254\tencounter with the same partner. Each conversation is released\n255\twith a human-made transcript and per-speaker audio recorded via\n256\theadset microphones.\n257\t\n258\t3.2\n259\t\n260\tLiking Scores and Evaluation Conditions\n261\t\n262\tAfter each conversation, both participants rated their partner using\n263\ta Japanese translation of the 13-item Rubin’s Liking Scale [18, 31].\n264\tEach item used a 1–9 Likert scale, and the scale had a Cronbach’s 𝛼\n265\tof .94. The prediction target is the liking score, defined as the mean\n266\tof the 13 items. The items span multiple facets of liking, including empathy, adaptability, trust in judgment, likability, perceived\n267\tsimilarity, intelligence, and admiration.\n268\tWe treat the two rating directions independently. F2M denotes\n269\tpredictions of liking scores assigned by female participants to their\n270\tmale partners, and M2F denotes the reverse. In both abbreviations,\n271\tthe direction runs from the rater to the rated partner. Within each\n272\tconversation group, we exclude a rater’s conversations if that rater\n273\tassigned the same liking score to every partner, because correlation\n274\tis undefined for that subset. After this exclusion, the Session 1\n275\tsamples contain 604 F2M pairs from 73 raters and 619 M2F pairs\n276\tfrom 72 raters. The Session 2 samples contain 619 pairs in each\n277\tdirection, from 75 female raters for F2M and 72 male raters for\n278\tM2F. Crossing the two conversation rounds with the two rating\n279\tdirections yields the four conditions analyzed throughout. Mean\n280\tliking scores ranged from 4.36 to 4.94 across the four conditions,\n281\twith standard deviations near 1.5.\n282\t\n283\t3.3\n284\t\n285\tTask Definition\n286\t\n287\t𝑖\n288\tLet D𝑖 = {(𝑋𝑖 𝑗 , 𝑦𝑖 𝑗 )}𝑁𝑗=1\n289\tdenote rater 𝑖’s set of conversations across\n290\tmultiple partners, the central unit of analysis. For each partner\n291\t𝑗, 𝑋𝑖 𝑗 denotes the transcript and/or speech information available\n292\tfrom the interaction, and 𝑦𝑖 𝑗 ∈ [1, 9] denotes the liking score that\n293\trater 𝑖 assigns to partner 𝑗 after the conversation. Our task is to\n294\tdevelop a predictor 𝑓 that maps 𝑋𝑖 𝑗 to a scalar score 𝑦ˆ𝑖 𝑗 = 𝑓 (𝑋𝑖 𝑗 ).\n295\tThe predictor should approximate the absolute liking score (𝑦ˆ𝑖 𝑗 ≈\n296\t𝑦𝑖 𝑗 ) while preserving the within-participant ordering of non-tied\n297\tpartner pairs. Preserving this ordering means that, for each non-tied\n298\t\n299\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n300\t\n301\tpair within a participant’s partner set, the predicted and observed\n302\tscore differences have the same sign.\n303\tRecovering within-participant differentiation is our primary inferential target: it captures which partners a participant reported\n304\tliking more relative to their own baseline, independent of betweenparticipant differences in response-scale use. Absolute score agreement nevertheless remains relevant when predictions are interpreted on the original 1–9 scale, motivating a calibration-sensitive\n305\toptimization objective. The supervised predictors are therefore\n306\ttrained on the concordance correlation coefficient (CCC) [22]. CCC\n307\trewards association only when the predicted and observed scores\n308\talso agree in location and scale.\n309\t\n310\t4\n311\t\n312\tModels, Fusion, and Evaluation Setup\n313\t\n314\tThe primary comparison combines a transcript-only LLM prediction from the full two-speaker transcript with a supervised prediction from rater-side speech. We use conventional weighted scorelevel late fusion to diagnose the speech predictor’s additional value,\n315\tnot as a new architecture. The supervised branches adapt Santana\n316\tet al.’s architecture [33]: frozen representations, additive attention\n317\tpooling, and modality-specific regression heads.\n318\t\n319\t4.1\n320\t\n321\tSupervised Single-Modality Predictors\n322\t\n323\tWe train separate supervised text and speech predictors rather than\n324\ta joint encoder. Each places a small learnable module on a frozen\n325\tpretrained encoder, so only the downstream aggregation and regression are learned from the speed-dating corpus. Both predictors\n326\tshare the same downstream architecture: attention pooling over\n327\tthe rater’s own utterances, followed by a scalar regression head.\n328\tHolding the downstream architecture constant helps isolate differences in what the encoders extract from the conversation. It also\n329\tmakes the two predictors directly compatible inputs to score-level\n330\tlate fusion. In what follows, we refer to each supervised predictor by the name of its underlying encoder (e.g., HuBERT denotes\n331\tthe supervised predictor built on frozen HuBERT features, not the\n332\tencoder alone).\n333\tText embeddings. Consecutive same-speaker transcript segments\n334\tare merged before utterance-level encoding. Each utterance is encoded with Sentence-T5-large (335M parameters) [27], yielding an\n335\tL2-normalized 768-dimensional sentence embedding.\n336\tSpeech embeddings. We use pretrained HuBERT-Large (317M\n337\tparameters) [15] as the speech encoder, a self-supervised speech\n338\tmodel pretrained on Libri-Light [19]. All parameters are frozen.\n339\tFor each utterance we feed the raw waveform (16 kHz) through\n340\tHuBERT and average-pool the final hidden states to obtain a 1,024dimensional embedding (L2-normalized). Utterances are cut by\n341\ttranscript timestamps with silence removed via WebRTC voice\n342\tactivity detection.\n343\tArchitecture details. The encoder stays frozen throughout training because each cross-validation training split contains only on\n344\tthe order of 102 training conversations, a regime in which finetuning a 108 -parameter model is prone to overfit. Utterance-level\n345\tembeddings are aggregated by additive attention pooling, letting\n346\tthe model emphasize utterances that carry rater-side signal about\n347\t\n348\tKikuchi et al.\n349\t\n350\tliking rather than weighting all utterances equally. Pooling is restricted to the rater’s own utterances; prior speed-dating work\n351\tshows that speakers modulate voice pitch with their own mate\n352\tpreferences, providing precedent for rater-side acoustic signal [29].\n353\tThe pooled representation passes through a two-layer head. A linear layer projects it to hidden dimension max(⌊𝑑/5⌋, 16) (where 𝑑\n354\tis the encoder output, 768 for text and 1024 for audio), followed\n355\tby ReLU and dropout 0.2; a final linear layer maps it to a scalar.\n356\tAdditional supervised baselines—BERT [9], the Japanese Linguistic\n357\tInquiry and Word Count (J-LIWC) [16], and openSMILE [11]—use\n358\tthe same architecture, with 𝑑 set to each feature’s dimension.\n359\tTraining settings. All supervised models use CCC loss (1 − CCC,\n360\twhere CCC is the mean per-participant CCC across raters in a\n361\tbatch). We optimize them with Adam (learning rate 10−3 , no weight\n362\tdecay or learning-rate scheduling). Batches include up to six raters\n363\tand all of their conversations. Training runs for a maximum of 500\n364\tepochs with early stopping (patience 20 on validation CCC) and\n365\trandom seed 42. Targets are standardized using the training-fold\n366\tmean and standard deviation; predictions are inverse-transformed\n367\tand clipped to the valid [1, 9] range.\n368\t\n369\t4.2\n370\t\n371\tLLM-Based Prediction\n372\t\n373\tTranscript-only LLM predictions provide the comparison point for\n374\ttesting the speech predictor’s additional value. We obtain them\n375\tusing zero-shot prompting, without task-specific fine-tuning or\n376\tin-context examples. Each model receives the complete chronological transcript, including utterances from both the rater and the\n377\tpartner, with speaker labels and timestamps; no rater-side filtering\n378\tis applied. The prompt identifies the rater (female for F2M, male for\n379\tM2F). Adapting Santana et al.’s item-wise questionnaire prediction\n380\tstrategy [33] to Rubin’s Liking Scale, the model predicts the rater’s\n381\tresponse to each of the scale’s 13 items on a 1–9 integer scale using\n382\teach API’s structured-output mechanism. The mean is the predicted\n383\tliking score (∈ [1, 9]). This input scope differs from the supervised\n384\ttext and speech branches, whose pooling is restricted to the rater’s\n385\town utterances. We use temperature 0 for all configurations reported in the main analysis. The prompt structure, Japanese and\n386\tEnglish versions, and placeholder substitutions are documented\n387\tin Section S2.42 ; the released code contains the exact executable\n388\ttemplates, including the full item wording.\n389\tTranscript-only LLMs. We evaluate proprietary LLMs (Claude\n390\tSonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.4) and an open-weight model (Gemma 3 12BIT) in a transcript-only (T) input configuration, where each model\n391\treceives this complete two-speaker transcript without audio. The\n392\tselected Claude configuration disables extended thinking.\n393\tMultimodal LLMs. We also evaluate an alternative route that\n394\tfeeds audio directly to the language model alongside the transcript.\n395\tAs auxiliary baselines, we use Gemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audiomini. Each model receives the full transcript and the full conversation audio in a mixed-mono input configuration created from the\n396\ttwo headset recordings. Gemini receives lossless FLAC at 16 kHz,\n397\twhereas GPT-audio-mini receives 128 kbps MP3 passed as base64.\n398\t2 In section, table, and figure numbers, “S” denotes the Supplementary Appendix.\n399\t\n400\t\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n401\t\n402\tFor Gemini, we report matched transcript-only (T) and text-plusaudio (T+A) input configurations. The two prompts are identical in\n403\ttranscript content, system role, and item-wise scoring instruction,\n404\tso the within-model difference isolates the addition of the audio\n405\tstream under this input configuration. For GPT-audio-mini, we\n406\treport the T+A configuration as a direct-input baseline and compare it with the transcript-only LLM baselines, but do not make a\n407\twithin-model audio ablation claim. Here, transcript-only LLM and\n408\tdirect-input MLLM describe the evaluated input configurations, not\n409\ta model’s underlying multimodal capability.\n410\t\n411\t4.3\n412\t\n413\tRepresentative Selection and Score-Level\n414\tLate Fusion\n415\t\n416\tWe perform score-level fusion of predictions from two unimodal\n417\trepresentatives, one per input branch. Score-level fusion is necessary for Claude because the branch exposes only a scalar score,\n418\tnot an intermediate embedding, making feature-level concatenation inapplicable. The supervised unimodal predictors likewise\n419\tproduce per-conversation scalar predictions. For the supervised\n420\ttext+speech pair (Sentence-T5 + HuBERT), where feature-level concatenation is technically feasible, Table 1 reports score-level late\n421\tand utterance-level early fusion as reference points. For the main fusion, we choose one representative per input branch a priori, before\n422\tinspecting any results: Claude for transcript-only prediction and\n423\tHuBERT for speech. Claude permits the common temperature-0\n424\tsetting, unlike extended-thinking mode. The broader comparison\n425\tpool additionally includes BERT, openSMILE, and J-LIWC as supervised baselines, alongside Sentence-T5 and the evaluated LLM and\n426\tMLLM variants. The retrospective complementarity landscape of\n427\tthis pool (Figure S1) is consistent with this choice: across all four\n428\tconditions, HuBERT occupies a favorable region of relatively high\n429\tutility and low prediction correlation with Claude.\n430\tGiven predictions 𝑦ˆ1 and 𝑦ˆ2 from the two branch representatives,\n431\tthe fused prediction is\n432\t𝑦ˆfusion = 𝑤 𝑦ˆ1 + (1 − 𝑤) 𝑦ˆ2,\n433\t\n434\t0 ≤ 𝑤 ≤ 1.\n435\t\n436\t(1)\n437\t\n438\tFor each test fold, the weight 𝑤 is selected by a 21-point grid search\n439\tover 𝑤 ∈ {0, 0.05, . . . , 1.0} to maximize per-participant CCC over\n440\tthe pooled held-out predictions of the remaining folds. The selected\n441\tweight is then applied to the held-out test fold without further\n442\ttuning. Because participants can appear in multiple conversation\n443\tgroups, the primary weight-selection pool is not strictly participantdisjoint; excluding all pairs involving test-fold participants yields\n444\tthe same qualitative and inferential conclusions (Section S2.3).\n445\t\n446\t4.4\n447\t\n448\tEvaluation Procedure\n449\t\n450\tWe evaluate predictions by 25-fold Leave-One-Group-Out crossvalidation, holding out each of the 25 conversation groups as the\n451\ttest fold. For the supervised models, all pairs involving participants\n452\tof the held-out group are removed from training, so the trained\n453\tpredictor never sees a test-fold participant. A separate validation\n454\tfold, participant-disjoint from the test fold, is used for early stopping.\n455\tRaters with fewer than three partners or near-constant ratings are\n456\tadditionally excluded from the training set only.\n457\tFor each participant, we compute Pearson’s 𝑟 between predicted\n458\tand true liking scores across their partners, then average 𝑟 across\n459\t\n460\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n461\t\n462\tparticipants. Per-participant 𝑟 is the primary evaluation metric\n463\tbecause our central research question concerns whether a model recovers each participant’s relative differentiation among partners. Its\n464\tinvariance to positive affine transformations factors out participantspecific location and scale use [7]. Optimization and primary evaluation use different criteria. CCC is calibration-sensitive: unlike 𝑟 , it\n465\tpenalizes differences in prediction mean and variance. We therefore\n466\tuse CCC for both supervised training and fusion-weight selection,\n467\tdiscouraging models or weights that preserve association while\n468\tproducing poorly calibrated scores on the original 1–9 scale. We\n469\tcompute CCC per participant and average it across participants, reporting it as a secondary measure of absolute agreement (Table S2).\n470\tWe also report per-participant Top-1 accuracy and pairwise accuracy (PW), the fraction of same-participant partner pairs whose\n471\tpredicted order matches ground truth. For Top-1, all partners tied\n472\tat the ground-truth maximum are valid; predicted ties receive fractional credit equal to the proportion belonging to that set. For PW,\n473\tpairs with tied ground-truth liking are excluded and tied predictions are counted as incorrect. PW depends only on ordering and\n474\tis therefore invariant to strictly increasing transformations.\n475\t\n476\t5 Results\n477\t5.1 Individual Models and Fusion Performance\n478\tTable 1 reports 𝑟 for four transcript-only LLMs, two text-plus-audio\n479\tMLLMs, five supervised unimodal models, and three fusion variants:\n480\ttwo score-level late-fusion variants and one utterance-level earlyfusion variant.\n481\tLLMs and auxiliary MLLM comparisons. The best-performing\n482\tLLM configuration in each condition is transcript-only: Claude\n483\tleads in Session 2 and GPT-5.4 leads in Session 1, although the\n484\tSession 1 differences are smaller than the SE reported for either\n485\tmodel. Under our mixed-mono input configuration, Gemini’s T+A\n486\tinput does not consistently improve on its matched transcript-only\n487\tinput, and GPT-audio-mini with T+A input does not surpass the\n488\tstrongest transcript-only baselines.\n489\tSupervised baselines and fusion variants. Claude has higher perparticipant 𝑟 than each of the three supervised text models, SentenceT5, BERT, and J-LIWC, in all four conditions (Wilcoxon signedrank tests; rank-biserial 𝑟 rb = .24–.62 over the twelve Claude vs.\n490\tsupervised-text comparisons). HuBERT is condition-dependent: its\n491\tperformance is closer to Claude’s in Session 2 than in Session 1,\n492\twhere it is substantially weaker. The openSMILE predictor produces near-constant outputs for a majority of Session 1 raters. Consequently, per-participant 𝑟 is undefined on the common rater set;\n493\tTable 1 marks these results as undefined with †. The score-level late\n494\tfusion of Sentence-T5 and HuBERT is below the Claude–HuBERT\n495\tfusion in all four conditions, so the Claude prediction is not interchangeable with the Sentence-T5 prediction under the shared fusion\n496\tsetup. The utterance-level early-fusion variant is lower than the\n497\tscore-level variant in three conditions; the two are essentially tied\n498\tin Session 1 F2M. These results establish Claude as the transcriptonly comparison point and HuBERT as a speech predictor whose\n499\tperformance varies by condition.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "961c1414-9afe-4616-8d97-4bbccb0657b8", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:50:55.170Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.23037v1.txt", "content": "In a direct study of speed dates, LLMs predicted objective and\nsubjective indicators of interaction success from conversation transcripts [24]. The other uses supervised models with lexical, speech,\ndialogue, and visual signals to predict social judgments and outcomes [30, 36]. Together, these lines raise a broad question about\nwhich conversational signals remain useful beyond transcript-only\nLLM prediction. We address a narrower question for speed-dating\nattraction: can a supervised speech predictor add predictive value\nbeyond a transcript-only LLM, and where does that value emerge?\nWe study this question using the Multi-Modal Speed Dating corpus [18], which contains Japanese speed-dating conversations with\ntranscripts, speaker-specific audio, and post-conversation liking\nscores. Our task is to predict each participant’s reported liking of\ntheir partners, with an emphasis on differentiation within the participant’s partner set. We define speech-derived complementarity as\nthe additional predictive value contributed by a supervised speech\npredictor beyond the evaluated transcript-only LLM’s predictions,\nwithout claiming that the speech predictor captures information\nfundamentally absent from transcripts.\n\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nFor the primary analysis, the transcript-only predictor is Claude\nSonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled (hereafter Claude), evaluated using zero-shot prompting. The supervised speech predictor is built on frozen HuBERT-Large representations (hereafter\nHuBERT). We combine their scalar predictions through weighted\nscore-level late fusion, as shown in Figure 1. This deliberately simple combination serves as a diagnostic for additional predictive\nvalue rather than as a new fusion architecture. We evaluate complementarity across two conversation rounds, two rating directions,\nand participants. Two multimodal LLMs that receive transcripts\nand audio provide auxiliary direct-input comparisons.\nIn summary, we clarify where a supervised speech predictor\nadds predictive value beyond the evaluated transcript-only LLM\nin speed-dating attraction prediction. We do so by examining this\nadded value across evaluation metrics, conversation rounds and\nrating directions, and participants, using score-level late fusion only\nas a diagnostic. Supporting analyses assess whether the transcriptonly LLM and speech predictor make redundant predictions by\nquantifying their within-rater prediction overlap and mutual incremental associations. Together, these analyses shift the question\nfrom whether speech helps on average to where its complementarity emerges.\nRelative to Claude, fusion significantly improves pairwise ranking accuracy in all four round-by-rating-direction settings. Gains\nin per-participant Pearson 𝑟 , however, vary across these settings,\nand no paired gain is statistically significant after correction. Retrospectively, participant-level 𝑟 gains are larger where HuBERT is\nmore accurate, although this association is partly expected because\nthe HuBERT prediction enters the fused score. The Claude and\nHuBERT predictions share little within-rater variance, with each\nexplaining comparable incremental in-sample variance beyond the\nother. As supporting context, Claude has higher per-participant 𝑟\nthan every tested supervised text model in all four settings.\nTaken together, these findings do not support a blanket claim that\nadding speech uniformly improves attraction prediction. Instead,\nthey show that speech can retain predictive value even when an\nLLM predicts attraction from transcripts. For multimodal social\nprediction, the relevant question is therefore not simply whether\nspeech helps, but where its complementarity emerges.\n\n2 Related Work\n2.1 LLMs for Interpersonal Inference\nBeyond attraction, LLMs have been evaluated as judges of empathic\ncommunication in text-based conversations. When given criteria\nfor each framework and expert-annotated examples, LLM–expert\nagreement approached expert–expert agreement across four evaluative frameworks [21].\nMost directly related to the present paper, Matz et al. [24] applied\nChatGPT to speed-dating transcripts to predict mutual contact exchange as well as subjective outcomes and experiences. ChatGPT’s\ncorrelations with these criteria were modest (𝑟 = .12–.23). For mutual contact exchange specifically, its performance was comparable\nto that of transcript-only human judges (𝑟 = .12 vs. .13). Building on this work, we test whether a supervised speech predictor\nadds predictive value beyond transcript-only LLM predictions for\ncontinuous liking and within-participant partner ranking.\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\n2.2\n\nMultimodal Prediction of Interpersonal\nOutcomes\n\nInterpersonal outcomes have long been studied computationally\nusing verbal and nonverbal features from conversation. Brief observations of expressive behavior can support accurate interpersonal\njudgments, and nonverbal communication encompasses cues in the\nface, voice, body, touch, and interpersonal space [2, 13]. In speed\ndating, supervised models have used prosodic, dialogue, and lexical\nfeatures to detect flirtation [30] and position, proximity, and motion features from video to predict contact exchange and physical\nattraction [36]. Complementary statistical analyses have linked\nconversational behavior to perceived connection in speed-dating\nencounters [25] and function-word language style matching to mutual romantic interest [17]. Speakers’ pitch convergence also varies\nwith their perception of the partner’s visual attractiveness and overall likability [26]. On Japanese speed-dating data, Ishii et al. [18]\npredicted post-interaction liking scores from information available\nbefore the interaction, including profiles, facial features, and psychometric measures. More recently, frozen self-supervised speech\nrepresentations, including HuBERT, have transferred effectively to\nparalinguistic tasks such as emotion recognition [15, 37].\nSantana et al. [33] provide the closest architectural precedent for\nour supervised branches. Their Speech-to-Joy framework trains separate predictors over frozen pretrained text and audio embeddings,\nusing additive attention pooling and modality-specific regression\nheads. The framework then averages the two predictors’ output\nscores for enjoyment prediction in human–robot dialogue. We adapt\nthis supervised-branch architecture, but our main comparison pairs\nthe speech predictor with a transcript-only LLM prediction rather\nthan a supervised text prediction.\n\n2.3\n\nLLM Integration in Multimodal Prediction\n\nMultimodal fusion methods are conventionally partitioned into\nfeature-level early fusion and score-level late fusion. Late fusion is\nparticularly well suited when the predictors are trained under different regimes, since each can be optimized independently. Recent\nwork has integrated LLMs into multimodal prediction pipelines\nin several ways. Some approaches textualize nonverbal modalities in the prompt [14, 23] or use an LLM for post hoc refinement\nof conventional model outputs [34]. Others use an LLM to integrate modality-specific predictions in natural language rather than\nthrough a weighted score-level combination [8].\nAt the task level, Pereira et al. [28] combined an LLM-derived\nenjoyment score with temporal, visual, and acoustic features in\nsupervised models for human–robot conversations, and separately\ncompared text-only and video-plus-audio LLM inputs. Their supervised target was third-party enjoyment annotations. The LLM\nscore entered the supervised model alongside modality descriptors\nrather than being fused at score level with the output of a separately\ntrained modality-specific predictor.\nTwo prior works more specifically combine score-level late fusion with LLMs, but neither treats a transcript-only LLM prediction as an independent branch. In Amin et al. [3], ChatGPT serves\n\n\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nFigure 1: Prediction pipeline for F2M (female rating male) and M2F (male rating female). Claude Sonnet 4.6, used with extended\nthinking disabled and zero-shot prompting, predicts liking from the full two-speaker transcript, while frozen HuBERT and a\nsupervised head predict from rater-side speech. Weighted score-level fusion combines the predictions using a fold-selected\nweight 𝑤.\nas an auxiliary text generator whose output is featurized alongside the raw text, so all fused branches take text input. In ZSFuse [20], whose “zero-shot large-scale model plus supervised specialist model” structure parallels ours, the zero-shot branch is itself\naudio-driven rather than transcript-driven. A separate direction\nintegrates audio directly into LLMs: audio-native models such as\nSALMONN [35] integrate pre-trained speech and audio encoders\nwith an LLM and let it reason over audio inputs directly. We include\nthis direction as an auxiliary comparison using the MLLM baselines\nGemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audio-mini with text-plus-audio input.\nTogether, these studies leave unresolved whether a separately\ntrained speech predictor improves interpersonal-attraction prediction beyond a transcript-only LLM. We treat a transcript-only\nLLM prediction obtained through zero-shot prompting and a separately trained supervised speech predictor as independent branches,\ncombining their outputs through weighted score-level late fusion.\nWe use this conventional fusion method to diagnose the speech\npredictor’s additional predictive value beyond the transcript-only\nprediction. Audio-native MLLMs form a separate direct-input route,\nwhich we include only as an auxiliary comparison in the present\nstudy.\n\n3 Corpus and Task Definition\n3.1 Speed-Dating Corpus\nWe use the Multi-Modal Speed Dating corpus [18]. It was collected\nin the speed-dating paradigm, a methodologically validated setting\nfor observing initial romantic attraction under controlled yet ecologically valid conditions [12]. The corpus records face-to-face conversations between male-female pairs involving 147 native Japanese\nspeakers: 75 female and 72 male participants. Their ages ranged\nfrom 19 to 60 years, with a mean of 31.9 years and a standard deviation of 8.6 years. All participants had indicated a strong interest\nin meeting future partners.\nParticipants were assigned to age-balanced groups of 5 female\nand 5 male members. Each group held all 25 possible male-female\npairwise conversations; some participants joined multiple groups.\nThe corpus is organized into two conversation rounds with nominal\ndurations of 5 minutes (Session 1) and 10 minutes (Session 2). Each\nsession contains 624 pairwise conversations for a total of 1,248.\n\nThe same pairs conversed in both rounds, and Session 2 was held\nafter Session 1. Thus, each Session 2 conversation followed a prior\nencounter with the same partner. Each conversation is released\nwith a human-made transcript and per-speaker audio recorded via\nheadset microphones.\n\n3.2\n\nLiking Scores and Evaluation Conditions\n\nAfter each conversation, both participants rated their partner using\na Japanese translation of the 13-item Rubin’s Liking Scale [18, 31].\nEach item used a 1–9 Likert scale, and the scale had a Cronbach’s 𝛼\nof .94. The prediction target is the liking score, defined as the mean\nof the 13 items. The items span multiple facets of liking, including empathy, adaptability, trust in judgment, likability, perceived\nsimilarity, intelligence, and admiration.\nWe treat the two rating directions independently. F2M denotes\npredictions of liking scores assigned by female participants to their\nmale partners, and M2F denotes the reverse. In both abbreviations,\nthe direction runs from the rater to the rated partner. Within each\nconversation group, we exclude a rater’s conversations if that rater\nassigned the same liking score to every partner, because correlation\nis undefined for that subset. After this exclusion, the Session 1\nsamples contain 604 F2M pairs from 73 raters and 619 M2F pairs\nfrom 72 raters. The Session 2 samples contain 619 pairs in each\ndirection, from 75 female raters for F2M and 72 male raters for\nM2F. Crossing the two conversation rounds with the two rating\ndirections yields the four conditions analyzed throughout. Mean\nliking scores ranged from 4.36 to 4.94 across the four conditions,\nwith standard deviations near 1.5.\n\n3.3\n\nTask Definition\n\n𝑖\nLet D𝑖 = {(𝑋𝑖 𝑗 , 𝑦𝑖 𝑗 )}𝑁𝑗=1\ndenote rater 𝑖’s set of conversations across\nmultiple partners, the central unit of analysis. For each partner\n𝑗, 𝑋𝑖 𝑗 denotes the transcript and/or speech information available\nfrom the interaction, and 𝑦𝑖 𝑗 ∈ [1, 9] denotes the liking score that\nrater 𝑖 assigns to partner 𝑗 after the conversation. Our task is to\ndevelop a predictor 𝑓 that maps 𝑋𝑖 𝑗 to a scalar score 𝑦ˆ𝑖 𝑗 = 𝑓 (𝑋𝑖 𝑗 ).\nThe predictor should approximate the absolute liking score (𝑦ˆ𝑖 𝑗 ≈\n𝑦𝑖 𝑗 ) while preserving the within-participant ordering of non-tied\npartner pairs. Preserving this ordering means that, for each non-tied\n\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\npair within a participant’s partner set, the predicted and observed\nscore differences have the same sign.\nRecovering within-participant differentiation is our primary inferential target: it captures which partners a participant reported\nliking more relative to their own baseline, independent of betweenparticipant differences in response-scale use. Absolute score agreement nevertheless remains relevant when predictions are interpreted on the original 1–9 scale, motivating a calibration-sensitive\noptimization objective. The supervised predictors are therefore\ntrained on the concordance correlation coefficient (CCC) [22]. CCC\nrewards association only when the predicted and observed scores\nalso agree in location and scale.\n\n4\n\nModels, Fusion, and Evaluation Setup\n\nThe primary comparison combines a transcript-only LLM prediction from the full two-speaker transcript with a supervised prediction from rater-side speech. We use conventional weighted scorelevel late fusion to diagnose the speech predictor’s additional value,\nnot as a new architecture. The supervised branches adapt Santana\net al.’s architecture [33]: frozen representations, additive attention\npooling, and modality-specific regression heads.\n\n4.1\n\nSupervised Single-Modality Predictors\n\nWe train separate supervised text and speech predictors rather than\na joint encoder. Each places a small learnable module on a frozen\npretrained encoder, so only the downstream aggregation and regression are learned from the speed-dating corpus. Both predictors\nshare the same downstream architecture: attention pooling over\nthe rater’s own utterances, followed by a scalar regression head.\nHolding the downstream architecture constant helps isolate differences in what the encoders extract from the conversation. It also\nmakes the two predictors directly compatible inputs to score-level\nlate fusion. In what follows, we refer to each supervised predictor by the name of its underlying encoder (e.g., HuBERT denotes\nthe supervised predictor built on frozen HuBERT features, not the\nencoder alone).\nText embeddings. Consecutive same-speaker transcript segments\nare merged before utterance-level encoding. Each utterance is encoded with Sentence-T5-large (335M parameters) [27], yielding an\nL2-normalized 768-dimensional sentence embedding.\nSpeech embeddings. We use pretrained HuBERT-Large (317M\nparameters) [15] as the speech encoder, a self-supervised speech\nmodel pretrained on Libri-Light [19]. All parameters are frozen.\nFor each utterance we feed the raw waveform (16 kHz) through\nHuBERT and average-pool the final hidden states to obtain a 1,024dimensional embedding (L2-normalized). Utterances are cut by\ntranscript timestamps with silence removed via WebRTC voice\nactivity detection.\nArchitecture details. The encoder stays frozen throughout training because each cross-validation training split contains only on\nthe order of 102 training conversations, a regime in which finetuning a 108 -parameter model is prone to overfit. Utterance-level\nembeddings are aggregated by additive attention pooling, letting\nthe model emphasize utterances that carry rater-side signal about\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\nliking rather than weighting all utterances equally. Pooling is restricted to the rater’s own utterances; prior speed-dating work\nshows that speakers modulate voice pitch with their own mate\npreferences, providing precedent for rater-side acoustic signal [29].\nThe pooled representation passes through a two-layer head. A linear layer projects it to hidden dimension max(⌊𝑑/5⌋, 16) (where 𝑑\nis the encoder output, 768 for text and 1024 for audio), followed\nby ReLU and dropout 0.2; a final linear layer maps it to a scalar.\nAdditional supervised baselines—BERT [9], the Japanese Linguistic\nInquiry and Word Count (J-LIWC) [16], and openSMILE [11]—use\nthe same architecture, with 𝑑 set to each feature’s dimension.\nTraining settings. All supervised models use CCC loss (1 − CCC,\nwhere CCC is the mean per-participant CCC across raters in a\nbatch). We optimize them with Adam (learning rate 10−3 , no weight\ndecay or learning-rate scheduling). Batches include up to six raters\nand all of their conversations. Training runs for a maximum of 500\nepochs with early stopping (patience 20 on validation CCC) and\nrandom seed 42. Targets are standardized using the training-fold\nmean and standard deviation; predictions are inverse-transformed\nand clipped to the valid [1, 9] range.\n\n4.2\n\nLLM-Based Prediction\n\nTranscript-only LLM predictions provide the comparison point for\ntesting the speech predictor’s additional value. We obtain them\nusing zero-shot prompting, without task-specific fine-tuning or\nin-context examples. Each model receives the complete chronological transcript, including utterances from both the rater and the\npartner, with speaker labels and timestamps; no rater-side filtering\nis applied. The prompt identifies the rater (female for F2M, male for\nM2F). Adapting Santana et al.’s item-wise questionnaire prediction\nstrategy [33] to Rubin’s Liking Scale, the model predicts the rater’s\nresponse to each of the scale’s 13 items on a 1–9 integer scale using\neach API’s structured-output mechanism. The mean is the predicted\nliking score (∈ [1, 9]). This input scope differs from the supervised\ntext and speech branches, whose pooling is restricted to the rater’s\nown utterances. We use temperature 0 for all configurations reported in the main analysis. The prompt structure, Japanese and\nEnglish versions, and placeholder substitutions are documented\nin Section S2.42 ; the released code contains the exact executable\ntemplates, including the full item wording.\nTranscript-only LLMs. We evaluate proprietary LLMs (Claude\nSonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.4) and an open-weight model (Gemma 3 12BIT) in a transcript-only (T) input configuration, where each model\nreceives this complete two-speaker transcript without audio. The\nselected Claude configuration disables extended thinking.\nMultimodal LLMs. We also evaluate an alternative route that\nfeeds audio directly to the language model alongside the transcript.\nAs auxiliary baselines, we use Gemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audiomini. Each model receives the full transcript and the full conversation audio in a mixed-mono input configuration created from the\ntwo headset recordings. Gemini receives lossless FLAC at 16 kHz,\nwhereas GPT-audio-mini receives 128 kbps MP3 passed as base64.\n2 In section, table, and figure numbers, “S” denotes the Supplementary Appendix.\n\n\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nFor Gemini, we report matched transcript-only (T) and text-plusaudio (T+A) input configurations. The two prompts are identical in\ntranscript content, system role, and item-wise scoring instruction,\nso the within-model difference isolates the addition of the audio\nstream under this input configuration. For GPT-audio-mini, we\nreport the T+A configuration as a direct-input baseline and compare it with the transcript-only LLM baselines, but do not make a\nwithin-model audio ablation claim. Here, transcript-only LLM and\ndirect-input MLLM describe the evaluated input configurations, not\na model’s underlying multimodal capability.\n\n4.3\n\nRepresentative Selection and Score-Level\nLate Fusion\n\nWe perform score-level fusion of predictions from two unimodal\nrepresentatives, one per input branch. Score-level fusion is necessary for Claude because the branch exposes only a scalar score,\nnot an intermediate embedding, making feature-level concatenation inapplicable. The supervised unimodal predictors likewise\nproduce per-conversation scalar predictions. For the supervised\ntext+speech pair (Sentence-T5 + HuBERT), where feature-level concatenation is technically feasible, Table 1 reports score-level late\nand utterance-level early fusion as reference points. For the main fusion, we choose one representative per input branch a priori, before\ninspecting any results: Claude for transcript-only prediction and\nHuBERT for speech. Claude permits the common temperature-0\nsetting, unlike extended-thinking mode. The broader comparison\npool additionally includes BERT, openSMILE, and J-LIWC as supervised baselines, alongside Sentence-T5 and the evaluated LLM and\nMLLM variants. The retrospective complementarity landscape of\nthis pool (Figure S1) is consistent with this choice: across all four\nconditions, HuBERT occupies a favorable region of relatively high\nutility and low prediction correlation with Claude.\nGiven predictions 𝑦ˆ1 and 𝑦ˆ2 from the two branch representatives,\nthe fused prediction is\n𝑦ˆfusion = 𝑤 𝑦ˆ1 + (1 − 𝑤) 𝑦ˆ2,\n\n0 ≤ 𝑤 ≤ 1.\n\n(1)\n\nFor each test fold, the weight 𝑤 is selected by a 21-point grid search\nover 𝑤 ∈ {0, 0.05, . . . , 1.0} to maximize per-participant CCC over\nthe pooled held-out predictions of the remaining folds. The selected\nweight is then applied to the held-out test fold without further\ntuning. Because participants can appear in multiple conversation\ngroups, the primary weight-selection pool is not strictly participantdisjoint; excluding all pairs involving test-fold participants yields\nthe same qualitative and inferential conclusions (Section S2.3).\n\n4.4\n\nEvaluation Procedure\n\nWe evaluate predictions by 25-fold Leave-One-Group-Out crossvalidation, holding out each of the 25 conversation groups as the\ntest fold. For the supervised models, all pairs involving participants\nof the held-out group are removed from training, so the trained\npredictor never sees a test-fold participant. A separate validation\nfold, participant-disjoint from the test fold, is used for early stopping.\nRaters with fewer than three partners or near-constant ratings are\nadditionally excluded from the training set only.\nFor each participant, we compute Pearson’s 𝑟 between predicted\nand true liking scores across their partners, then average 𝑟 across\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nparticipants. Per-participant 𝑟 is the primary evaluation metric\nbecause our central research question concerns whether a model recovers each participant’s relative differentiation among partners. Its\ninvariance to positive affine transformations factors out participantspecific location and scale use [7]. Optimization and primary evaluation use different criteria. CCC is calibration-sensitive: unlike 𝑟 , it\npenalizes differences in prediction mean and variance. We therefore\nuse CCC for both supervised training and fusion-weight selection,\ndiscouraging models or weights that preserve association while\nproducing poorly calibrated scores on the original 1–9 scale. We\ncompute CCC per participant and average it across participants, reporting it as a secondary measure of absolute agreement (Table S2).\nWe also report per-participant Top-1 accuracy and pairwise accuracy (PW), the fraction of same-participant partner pairs whose\npredicted order matches ground truth. For Top-1, all partners tied\nat the ground-truth maximum are valid; predicted ties receive fractional credit equal to the proportion belonging to that set. For PW,\npairs with tied ground-truth liking are excluded and tied predictions are counted as incorrect. PW depends only on ordering and\nis therefore invariant to strictly increasing transformations.\n\n5 Results\n5.1 Individual Models and Fusion Performance\nTable 1 reports 𝑟 for four transcript-only LLMs, two text-plus-audio\nMLLMs, five supervised unimodal models, and three fusion variants:\ntwo score-level late-fusion variants and one utterance-level earlyfusion variant.\nLLMs and auxiliary MLLM comparisons. The best-performing\nLLM configuration in each condition is transcript-only: Claude\nleads in Session 2 and GPT-5.4 leads in Session 1, although the\nSession 1 differences are smaller than the SE reported for either\nmodel. Under our mixed-mono input configuration, Gemini’s T+A\ninput does not consistently improve on its matched transcript-only\ninput, and GPT-audio-mini with T+A input does not surpass the\nstrongest transcript-only baselines.\nSupervised baselines and fusion variants. Claude has higher perparticipant 𝑟 than each of the three supervised text models, SentenceT5, BERT, and J-LIWC, in all four conditions (Wilcoxon signedrank tests; rank-biserial 𝑟 rb = .24–.62 over the twelve Claude vs.\nsupervised-text comparisons). HuBERT is condition-dependent: its\nperformance is closer to Claude’s in Session 2 than in Session 1,\nwhere it is substantially weaker. The openSMILE predictor produces near-constant outputs for a majority of Session 1 raters. Consequently, per-participant 𝑟 is undefined on the common rater set;\nTable 1 marks these results as undefined with †. The score-level late\nfusion of Sentence-T5 and HuBERT is below the Claude–HuBERT\nfusion in all four conditions, so the Claude prediction is not interchangeable with the Sentence-T5 prediction under the shared fusion\nsetup. The utterance-level early-fusion variant is lower than the\nscore-level variant in three conditions; the two are essentially tied\nin Session 1 F2M. These results establish Claude as the transcriptonly comparison point and HuBERT as a speech predictor whose\nperformance varies by condition.", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 100, "totalLines": 2171}}}
