{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b2053decee71cbcd", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\t\n501\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n502\t\n503\tKikuchi et al.\n504\t\n505\tTable 1: Per-participant Pearson correlation (𝑟 ) for transcriptonly LLMs, direct-input text-plus-audio MLLMs, supervised\n506\tunimodal predictors, and fusion variants. S1/S2 denote Sessions 1/2; F2M denotes female participants rating male partners, and M2F the reverse.\n507\tModel / configuration\n508\t\n509\tS1 F2M S1 M2F\n510\t\n511\tS2 F2M\n512\t\n513\tS2 M2F\n514\t\n515\tLLMs (T)\n516\tClaude\n517\tGemini 2.5 Flash\n518\tGPT-5.4\n519\tGemma 3 12B-IT\n520\t\n521\t.248±.048 .163±.044\n522\t.223±.055 .183±.048\n523\t.256±.045 .203±.054\n524\t.060±.050 .122±.047\n525\t\n526\t.244±.050\n527\t.154±.053\n528\t.196±.048\n529\t.052±.053\n530\t\n531\t.238±.044\n532\t.222±.044\n533\t.237±.043\n534\t.027±.048\n535\t\n536\tMLLMs (T+A)\n537\tGemini 2.5 Flash\n538\tGPT-audio-mini\n539\t\n540\t.162±.051 .116±.050 .168±.041\n541\t.044±.044 .135±.048 .149±.049\n542\t\n543\t.102±.044\n544\t.193±.045\n545\t\n546\tSupervised text predictors\n547\tSentence-T5\n548\tBERT\n549\tJ-LIWC\n550\t\n551\t.057±.059 .014±.053 .101±.051 .018±.052\n552\t.044±.057 .076±.042 −.001±.049 .051±.052\n553\t.121±.042 .031±.050 .046±.057 −.077±.048\n554\t\n555\tSupervised speech predictors\n556\tHuBERT\n557\topenSMILE\n558\t\n559\t.105±.052 .092±.054 .212±.048\n560\t†\n561\t†\n562\t.006±.061\n563\t\n564\t.183±.043\n565\t.088±.051\n566\t\n567\tSupervised text + speech fusion\n568\tSentence-T5 + HuBERT (L)\n569\t.103±.052 .097±.055 .201±.048\n570\tSentence-T5 + HuBERT (E)\n571\t.106±.050 .029±.049 .173±.049\n572\t\n573\t.180±.043\n574\t.147±.046\n575\t\n576\tLLM + speech fusion\n577\tClaude + HuBERT (L)\n578\t\n579\t.248±.044\n580\t\n581\t.281±.049 .164±.046 .323±.044\n582\t\n583\tNote. Values are mean per-participant 𝑟 ± standard error of the mean (SE); undefined 𝑟\n584\tvalues are excluded. Bold marks the highest point estimate in each column. † denotes\n585\t𝑟 undefined for openSMILE in Session 1 because of near-constant predictions. L and E\n586\tdenote weighted score-level late fusion and utterance-level early fusion, respectively.\n587\tModels. Claude: Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled; Sentence-T5:\n588\tSentence-T5-large; HuBERT: HuBERT-Large.\n589\t\n590\t5.2\n591\t\n592\tFusion Analysis\n593\t\n594\tCondition-dependent fusion gain. Throughout, we define fusion\n595\tgain relative to Claude as Δ = Fusion − Claude, using Δ𝑟 for perparticipant 𝑟 and Δ PW for pairwise accuracy. Relative to Claude,\n596\tthe Claude–HuBERT fusion significantly improves pairwise accuracy in all four conditions, with Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05 in each\n597\t(Table 2). By contrast, Δ𝑟 is largest in Session 2 F2M, smaller in Session 1 F2M, and essentially zero in both M2F conditions (Table 1);\n598\tno paired Δ𝑟 reaches significance after Holm correction. The fused\n599\tmodel also exceeds HuBERT alone in pairwise accuracy in three\n600\tof four conditions, with Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05 in each. Session 1\n601\tM2F is the exception: HuBERT is the stronger single predictor, and\n602\tfusion does not improve on HuBERT. The selected weights are comparable between Claude and HuBERT in Session 2 but concentrate\n603\ton Claude in Session 1 (Figure S2).\n604\tReplacing Claude with GPT-5.4 yields a similar four-condition\n605\tΔ𝑟 pattern (Table S7). This robustness does not extend to the tested\n606\talternative representatives: substituting Sentence-T5 for Claude or\n607\topenSMILE for HuBERT lowers fusion per-participant 𝑟 in every\n608\tcondition (Table S1). Table 3 further shows little shared withinrater explained variance between Claude and HuBERT, with each\n609\tproviding a similar positive increment beyond the other.\n610\t\n611\tPairwise accuracy by rank distance. Aggregate PW shows that\n612\tfusion improves pairwise ordering over Claude, but not where\n613\tthe gains occur. Table 2 therefore breaks down fusion gains by\n614\twithin-participant rank distance using Near (1–2), Mid (3–5), and\n615\tFar (6+) bins, and reports Top-1 change separately. In Session 2\n616\tF2M, where the 𝑟 gain is largest, Δ PW increases with rank distance;\n617\tthis pattern is less clear elsewhere. Tie-aware Top-1 changes are\n618\tsmall, decreasing slightly in Session 1 and increasing slightly in\n619\tSession 2, so aggregate PW gains do not correspond to uniform\n620\timprovements in identifying the top-ranked partner.\n621\t\n622\t6 Discussion\n623\t6.1 When Does Speech Complement the\n624\tTranscript-Only LLM?\n625\tSpeech-derived complementarity is conditional across evaluation\n626\tmetrics, conditions, and participants. Relative to Claude, fusion\n627\tsignificantly improves PW in all four conditions (Table 2), indicating\n628\tmore accurate ordering of partner pairs. By contrast, no paired Δ𝑟\n629\treaches significance after correction, so the results do not show a\n630\tuniform improvement in participants’ overall differentiation among\n631\tpartners. Absolute per-participant 𝑟 also remains modest for all\n632\tmodels; the gains indicate complementarity, not deployment-ready\n633\taccuracy.\n634\tAt the condition level, HuBERT is closer to Claude in Session 2\n635\tthan in Session 1, and the selected fusion weights are correspondingly more balanced in Session 2 (Figure S2). Stronger HuBERT\n636\tperformance does not, however, map uniformly onto larger 𝑟 gains:\n637\tthe largest numerical gain occurs in Session 2 F2M, but the two\n638\tM2F conditions show essentially no change. HuBERT’s stronger\n639\tSession 2 performance may reflect both prior-encounter exposure\n640\tand longer within-encounter observation, which this corpus cannot disentangle. Pitch convergence in speed dating varies with\n641\tperceived partner attractiveness [26], and longer interaction may\n642\tpermit richer partner representations [1]. The observed condition\n643\tpattern therefore locates, but does not explain, where complementarity emerges.\n644\tFigure 2 shows the participant-level relationship between HuBERT’s 𝑟 and fusion gain Δ𝑟 . Across participants, Claude’s 𝑟 and\n645\tHuBERT’s 𝑟 are weakly associated (|𝜌 | < .30 in all conditions),\n646\twhereas HuBERT’s 𝑟 is strongly associated with Δ𝑟 in every condition (𝜌 ≥ .70, all 𝑝 < .0001). Because Δ𝑟 is computed from a fused\n647\tprediction that already contains the HuBERT score, the correlation\n648\tbetween HuBERT’s 𝑟 and Δ𝑟 is partly structurally expected and is\n649\tnot an independent test of a participant-level mechanism. The correlation instead provides a descriptive localization of the observed\n650\tgains: participants for whom HuBERT is weak also show little\n651\tfusion benefit. Prior multimodal affect research has emphasized\n652\taggregate fusion gains over unimodal baselines [10, 33], leaving\n653\tsuch participant-level variation largely unexplored.\n654\tA within-rater variance decomposition further characterizes the\n655\toverlap between the Claude and HuBERT predictions (Table 3).\n656\tThe two predictions are weakly correlated within raters (mean 𝑟\n657\tbetween −.08 and +.13), and their shared 𝑅 2 is at most .04 in every\n658\tcondition. The incremental associations are comparable in both\n659\tdirections, indicating mutual rather than one-sided association.\n660\tThe low shared 𝑅 2 and comparable bidirectional increments are\n661\t\n662\t\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n663\t\n664\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n665\t\n666\tTable 2: Pairwise accuracy (PW) and fusion gains over Claude, reported overall and by rank distance, together with changes in\n667\tTop-1 accuracy. S1/S2 denote Sessions 1/2; F2M denotes female participants rating male partners, and M2F the reverse.\n668\tAggregate PW\n669\t\n670\tFusion comparison with Claude\n671\t\n672\tCondition Claude HuBERT Fusion Overall PW gain\n673\tS1 F2M\n674\tS1 M2F\n675\tS2 F2M\n676\tS2 M2F\n677\t\n678\t.549\n679\t.518\n680\t.579\n681\t.545\n682\t\n683\t.531\n684\t.560\n685\t.597\n686\t.564\n687\t\n688\t.603\n689\t.543\n690\t.628\n691\t.594\n692\t\n693\t+.054\n694\t+.025\n695\t+.049\n696\t+.049\n697\t\n698\t𝑟 rb\n699\t\n700\tNear PW gain Mid PW gain Far PW gain Top-1 change\n701\t\n702\t.594∗∗∗\n703\t.623∗∗\n704\t.299∗\n705\t.374∗\n706\t\n707\t+.044\n708\t+.026\n709\t+.031\n710\t+.044\n711\t\n712\t+.076\n713\t+.039\n714\t+.055\n715\t+.042\n716\t\n717\t+.074\n718\t+.031\n719\t+.091\n720\t+.050\n721\t\n722\t−.025\n723\t−.007\n724\t+.013\n725\t+.028\n726\t\n727\tNote. Claude denotes Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled. Aggregate PW (left) is macro-averaged across participants; rank-distance bins (Near = 1–2, Mid = 3–5, Far\n728\t= 6+) are micro-averaged over all within-participant pairs (rank distance is a pair-level attribute), so bins need not average to the aggregate. PW gain and Top-1 change are\n729\tcomputed as Fusion − Claude. 𝑟 rb is the rank-biserial effect size for the Fusion vs. Claude Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Bold marks the bin with the largest PW gain per condition.\n730\t∗ 𝑝 < .05, ∗∗ 𝑝 < .01, ∗∗∗ 𝑝 < .001 (Holm-corrected, 𝑚 = 4).\n731\t\n732\tTable 3: Mutual incremental within-rater variance between\n733\tClaude and HuBERT across the four conditions.\n734\tIncremental 𝑅 2\n735\tCondition\n736\tS1 F2M\n737\tS1 M2F\n738\tS2 F2M\n739\tS2 M2F\n740\t\n741\tHuBERT beyond Claude beyond\n742\tClaude\n743\tHuBERT\n744\tShared 𝑅 2\n745\t+.17 [.13, .22]\n746\t+.20 [.15, .26]\n747\t+.21 [.16, .26]\n748\t+.14 [.11, .17]\n749\t\n750\t+.19 [.15, .24]\n751\t+.15 [.11, .19]\n752\t+.24 [.19, .29]\n753\t+.17 [.13, .21]\n754\t\n755\t.04\n756\t.01\n757\t.01\n758\t.03\n759\t\n760\tNote. Claude denotes Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled. “HuBERT\n761\tbeyond Claude” is the increase in 𝑅 2 obtained by adding the HuBERT prediction to the\n762\tClaude prediction; “Claude beyond HuBERT” is defined symmetrically. Shared 𝑅 2 is\n763\tthe overlap in explained variance. Values are within-rater, in-sample averages (hence\n764\tthe increments are ≥ 0); brackets show 95% CIs based on 2,000 participant bootstraps.\n765\t\n766\tconsistent with limited overlap between the two predictions, but the\n767\tdecomposition does not by itself distinguish distinct information\n768\tfrom variance reduction under noisy prediction.\n769\t\n770\t6.2\n771\t\n772\tLate Fusion vs. Direct Multimodal Input\n773\t\n774\tThe auxiliary MLLM comparison examines a different route for\n775\tincorporating speech. Under the tested mixed-mono configuration,\n776\tGemini’s text-plus-audio input does not consistently improve on\n777\tits matched transcript-only input, and GPT-audio-mini does not\n778\tsurpass the strongest transcript-only baselines. By contrast, combining independently obtained Claude and HuBERT predictions\n779\tyields a pairwise-ordering benefit in the present configuration. The\n780\tobserved contrast between direct multimodal input and score-level\n781\tfusion does not establish a general advantage of score-level late\n782\tfusion over direct multimodal inference; the comparison is specific\n783\tto the evaluated models, prompts, and audio processing.\n784\tScore-level fusion instead provides a modular way to evaluate the speech predictor’s additional predictive value relative to\n785\ta transcript-only LLM. The selected weights place less weight on\n786\tHuBERT in Session 1, where it is less accurate, and relatively more\n787\tweight on it in Session 2. As transcript-only LLMs evolve, the magnitude of speech-derived complementarity remains an empirical\n788\tquestion across models and conditions.\n789\t\n790\t6.3\n791\t\n792\tLimitations and Future Work\n793\t\n794\tEvaluation target and partner-level consensus. Per-participant 𝑟\n795\tevaluates within-participant differentiation among partners but\n796\tdoes not distinguish rater-specific partner preference from partnerlevel consensus shared across raters. To contextualize this limitation, we construct a descriptive partner-level consensus reference\n797\tusing the mean score each partner received from other raters. The\n798\treference reaches per-participant 𝑟 of .36–.48, above the Claude–\n799\tHuBERT fusion in all four conditions (Section S1.6). These groundtruth ratings are unavailable at inference, so the reference provides\n800\tcontext rather than a deployable baseline.\n801\tMechanism of speech-derived complementarity. Figure 2 shows\n802\tthat fusion gains are concentrated among participants for whom\n803\tHuBERT has higher 𝑟 , but this association is retrospective: it requires all conversations from a participant and cannot predict fusion\n804\tbenefit in advance. Table 3 characterizes the mutual incremental\n805\tassociations between Claude and HuBERT. Replacing HuBERT with\n806\tthe hand-crafted openSMILE representation does not improve on\n807\tClaude in any condition (Table S1). These analyses do not identify\n808\twhich acoustic information HuBERT captures. HuBERT represents\n809\tsequential speech structure [15] and transfers well to paralinguistic\n810\ttasks [37]. Which cues contribute—laughter, pauses, backchannels,\n811\tor turn-taking—remains open. Future work should test whether\n812\tparticipant-adaptive or hierarchical models can prospectively estimate speech-branch reliability.\n813\tDirection asymmetry and conversation round. Numerical Δ𝑟 differs across rating directions and is largest in Session 2 F2M. Because\n814\tSession 2 combines prior-encounter exposure with a longer nominal duration, this corpus cannot separate their contributions. Future\n815\tstudies should vary these factors independently. Modeling actor\n816\tand partner effects, interpersonal synchrony, and turn-level dynamics such as prosodic adaptation may also help explain the observed\n817\tcondition dependence.\n818\tCorpus, language, and inclusivity. The evidence comes from a single Japanese speed-dating corpus with 147 participants, restricted\n819\tto opposite-sex pairs. Whether the condition-dependent complementarity we observe generalizes across languages and pair compositions or to other short, dyadic, first-encounter interactions with\n820\tan explicit evaluative goal remains an open empirical question.\n821\t\n822\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n823\t\n824\tKikuchi et al.\n825\t\n826\tFigure 2: Across all four conditions, participants with higher HuBERT per-participant Pearson 𝑟 show larger Claude–HuBERT\n827\tfusion gain Δ𝑟 over Claude, although gain magnitude varies by condition. Panels show Sessions 1/2 and female-to-male\n828\t(F2M)/male-to-female (M2F) ratings. Points are participants; solid lines and bands show OLS fits and 95% CIs, and dashed lines\n829\tmark zero. Because HuBERT enters the fused score, this association is descriptive rather than an independent mechanism.\n830\tModel and modality coverage. The main analysis focuses on text\n831\tand speech, although video cues can also predict interpersonal\n832\toutcomes in speed dating [36]. The direct-input MLLM comparison covers two models under one prompting and audio-processing\n833\tpipeline; audio-LLM capabilities are evolving rapidly [32]. We reuse\n834\tthe transcript-only prompt, mix the headset recordings to mono,\n835\tand supply GPT-audio-mini with compressed MP3 audio. The supervised branches likewise use HuBERT and Sentence-T5 rather\n836\tthan exhaustively comparing alternatives such as wav2vec 2.0 [4],\n837\tWavLM [6], or Japanese and multilingual encoders. These choices\n838\tbound the comparison: the experiments neither establish the general performance of direct-input MLLMs nor determine which\n839\tspeech encoder best captures Japanese paralinguistic cues. Future work should evaluate video-aware extensions, audio-specific\n840\tprompts, speaker-separated audio, and alternative encoders.\n841\t\n842\t7\n843\t\n844\tConclusion\n845\t\n846\tIn Japanese speed dating, we examined whether a supervised speech\n847\tpredictor complements a transcript-only LLM in predicting how\n848\tmuch participants reported liking each partner. The results show\n849\tthat speech can complement transcript-only LLM prediction, but\n850\tnot as a uniform multimodal gain. Across both conversation rounds\n851\tand rating directions, fusing speech and transcript-only LLM predictions significantly improves pairwise ranking accuracy over the\n852\ttranscript-only LLM alone. By contrast, gains in per-participant\n853\tPearson 𝑟 vary across these settings, and none is significant after\n854\tcorrection. Retrospectively, these 𝑟 gains are larger for participants\n855\twhose liking is more accurately predicted by the speech predictor.\n856\tSpeech can therefore retain predictive value even when an LLM\n857\tpredicts interpersonal attraction from conversation transcripts. For\n858\tmultimodal social prediction, the relevant question is not simply\n859\twhether speech helps, but how its complementarity varies across\n860\tevaluation metrics, conversation settings, and participants.\n861\t\n862\tspeed-dating corpus was collected under institutional ethics approval and written informed consent, with access restricted to\n863\tcontract-based research. The main analysis uses transcripts and\n864\taudio; supplementary checks also use overhead video. These data\n865\tremain identifying and sensitive. Predictors may reflect pretraining\n866\tbiases, and findings apply only to Japanese opposite-sex speed dating. Responsible deployment requires explicit consent, bias auditing,\n867\thuman oversight, and validation across broader gender identities\n868\tand cultures.3\n869\t\n870\tAcknowledgments\n871\tThis work was partially supported by JSPS KAKENHI Grant Number 26K03016, JST CREST Grant Number JPMJCR2563, JST CRONOS\n872\tGrant Number JPMJCS24K7, and AMED under Grant Number\n873\tJP256f0137001.\n874\t\n875\tReferences\n876\t[1] Irwin Altman and Dalmas A. Taylor. 1973. Social Penetration: The Development of\n877\tInterpersonal Relationships. Holt, Rinehart and Winston, New York.\n878\t[2] Nalini Ambady and Robert Rosenthal. 1992. Thin Slices of Expressive Behavior\n879\tas Predictors of Interpersonal Consequences: A Meta-Analysis. Psychological\n880\tBulletin 111, 2 (1992), 256–274. doi:10.1037/0033-2909.111.2.256\n881\t[3] Mostafa M. Amin, Erik Cambria, and Björn W. Schuller. 2023. Can ChatGPT’s\n882\tResponses Boost Traditional Natural Language Processing? IEEE Intelligent\n883\tSystems 38, 5 (2023), 5–11. doi:10.1109/MIS.2023.3305861\n884\t[4] Alexei Baevski, Yuhao Zhou, Abdelrahman Mohamed, and Michael Auli. 2020.\n885\twav2vec 2.0: A Framework for Self-Supervised Learning of Speech Representations. In Proc. Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS),\n886\tH. Larochelle, M. Ranzato, R. Hadsell, M.F. Balcan, and H. Lin (Eds.), Vol. 33.\n887\tCurran Associates, Inc., 12449–12460. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_\n888\tfiles/paper/2020/file/92d1e1eb1cd6f9fba3227870bb6d7f07-Paper.pdf\n889\t[5] Ellen Berscheid and Harry T. Reis. 1998. Attraction and Close Relationships. In\n890\tThe Handbook of Social Psychology (4 ed.), Daniel T. Gilbert, Susan T. Fiske, and\n891\tGardner Lindzey (Eds.). Vol. 2. McGraw-Hill, Boston, 193–281.\n892\t[6] Sanyuan Chen, Chengyi Wang, Zhengyang Chen, Yu Wu, Shujie Liu, Zhuo Chen,\n893\tJinyu Li, Naoyuki Kanda, Takuya Yoshioka, Xiong Xiao, Jian Wu, Long Zhou,\n894\tShuo Ren, Yanmin Qian, Yao Qian, Jian Wu, Michael Zeng, Xiangzhan Yu, and\n895\tFuru Wei. 2022. WavLM: Large-Scale Self-Supervised Pre-Training for Full Stack\n896\tSpeech Processing. IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing 16, 6 (2022),\n897\t1505–1518. doi:10.1109/JSTSP.2022.3188113\n898\t\n899\tSafe and Responsible Innovation Statement\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "415a56e3-9441-46c3-a868-701ba8e3108a", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:51:10.094Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.23037v1.txt", "content": "\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\nTable 1: Per-participant Pearson correlation (𝑟 ) for transcriptonly LLMs, direct-input text-plus-audio MLLMs, supervised\nunimodal predictors, and fusion variants. S1/S2 denote Sessions 1/2; F2M denotes female participants rating male partners, and M2F the reverse.\nModel / configuration\n\nS1 F2M S1 M2F\n\nS2 F2M\n\nS2 M2F\n\nLLMs (T)\nClaude\nGemini 2.5 Flash\nGPT-5.4\nGemma 3 12B-IT\n\n.248±.048 .163±.044\n.223±.055 .183±.048\n.256±.045 .203±.054\n.060±.050 .122±.047\n\n.244±.050\n.154±.053\n.196±.048\n.052±.053\n\n.238±.044\n.222±.044\n.237±.043\n.027±.048\n\nMLLMs (T+A)\nGemini 2.5 Flash\nGPT-audio-mini\n\n.162±.051 .116±.050 .168±.041\n.044±.044 .135±.048 .149±.049\n\n.102±.044\n.193±.045\n\nSupervised text predictors\nSentence-T5\nBERT\nJ-LIWC\n\n.057±.059 .014±.053 .101±.051 .018±.052\n.044±.057 .076±.042 −.001±.049 .051±.052\n.121±.042 .031±.050 .046±.057 −.077±.048\n\nSupervised speech predictors\nHuBERT\nopenSMILE\n\n.105±.052 .092±.054 .212±.048\n†\n†\n.006±.061\n\n.183±.043\n.088±.051\n\nSupervised text + speech fusion\nSentence-T5 + HuBERT (L)\n.103±.052 .097±.055 .201±.048\nSentence-T5 + HuBERT (E)\n.106±.050 .029±.049 .173±.049\n\n.180±.043\n.147±.046\n\nLLM + speech fusion\nClaude + HuBERT (L)\n\n.248±.044\n\n.281±.049 .164±.046 .323±.044\n\nNote. Values are mean per-participant 𝑟 ± standard error of the mean (SE); undefined 𝑟\nvalues are excluded. Bold marks the highest point estimate in each column. † denotes\n𝑟 undefined for openSMILE in Session 1 because of near-constant predictions. L and E\ndenote weighted score-level late fusion and utterance-level early fusion, respectively.\nModels. Claude: Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled; Sentence-T5:\nSentence-T5-large; HuBERT: HuBERT-Large.\n\n5.2\n\nFusion Analysis\n\nCondition-dependent fusion gain. Throughout, we define fusion\ngain relative to Claude as Δ = Fusion − Claude, using Δ𝑟 for perparticipant 𝑟 and Δ PW for pairwise accuracy. Relative to Claude,\nthe Claude–HuBERT fusion significantly improves pairwise accuracy in all four conditions, with Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05 in each\n(Table 2). By contrast, Δ𝑟 is largest in Session 2 F2M, smaller in Session 1 F2M, and essentially zero in both M2F conditions (Table 1);\nno paired Δ𝑟 reaches significance after Holm correction. The fused\nmodel also exceeds HuBERT alone in pairwise accuracy in three\nof four conditions, with Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05 in each. Session 1\nM2F is the exception: HuBERT is the stronger single predictor, and\nfusion does not improve on HuBERT. The selected weights are comparable between Claude and HuBERT in Session 2 but concentrate\non Claude in Session 1 (Figure S2).\nReplacing Claude with GPT-5.4 yields a similar four-condition\nΔ𝑟 pattern (Table S7). This robustness does not extend to the tested\nalternative representatives: substituting Sentence-T5 for Claude or\nopenSMILE for HuBERT lowers fusion per-participant 𝑟 in every\ncondition (Table S1). Table 3 further shows little shared withinrater explained variance between Claude and HuBERT, with each\nproviding a similar positive increment beyond the other.\n\nPairwise accuracy by rank distance. Aggregate PW shows that\nfusion improves pairwise ordering over Claude, but not where\nthe gains occur. Table 2 therefore breaks down fusion gains by\nwithin-participant rank distance using Near (1–2), Mid (3–5), and\nFar (6+) bins, and reports Top-1 change separately. In Session 2\nF2M, where the 𝑟 gain is largest, Δ PW increases with rank distance;\nthis pattern is less clear elsewhere. Tie-aware Top-1 changes are\nsmall, decreasing slightly in Session 1 and increasing slightly in\nSession 2, so aggregate PW gains do not correspond to uniform\nimprovements in identifying the top-ranked partner.\n\n6 Discussion\n6.1 When Does Speech Complement the\nTranscript-Only LLM?\nSpeech-derived complementarity is conditional across evaluation\nmetrics, conditions, and participants. Relative to Claude, fusion\nsignificantly improves PW in all four conditions (Table 2), indicating\nmore accurate ordering of partner pairs. By contrast, no paired Δ𝑟\nreaches significance after correction, so the results do not show a\nuniform improvement in participants’ overall differentiation among\npartners. Absolute per-participant 𝑟 also remains modest for all\nmodels; the gains indicate complementarity, not deployment-ready\naccuracy.\nAt the condition level, HuBERT is closer to Claude in Session 2\nthan in Session 1, and the selected fusion weights are correspondingly more balanced in Session 2 (Figure S2). Stronger HuBERT\nperformance does not, however, map uniformly onto larger 𝑟 gains:\nthe largest numerical gain occurs in Session 2 F2M, but the two\nM2F conditions show essentially no change. HuBERT’s stronger\nSession 2 performance may reflect both prior-encounter exposure\nand longer within-encounter observation, which this corpus cannot disentangle. Pitch convergence in speed dating varies with\nperceived partner attractiveness [26], and longer interaction may\npermit richer partner representations [1]. The observed condition\npattern therefore locates, but does not explain, where complementarity emerges.\nFigure 2 shows the participant-level relationship between HuBERT’s 𝑟 and fusion gain Δ𝑟 . Across participants, Claude’s 𝑟 and\nHuBERT’s 𝑟 are weakly associated (|𝜌 | < .30 in all conditions),\nwhereas HuBERT’s 𝑟 is strongly associated with Δ𝑟 in every condition (𝜌 ≥ .70, all 𝑝 < .0001). Because Δ𝑟 is computed from a fused\nprediction that already contains the HuBERT score, the correlation\nbetween HuBERT’s 𝑟 and Δ𝑟 is partly structurally expected and is\nnot an independent test of a participant-level mechanism. The correlation instead provides a descriptive localization of the observed\ngains: participants for whom HuBERT is weak also show little\nfusion benefit. Prior multimodal affect research has emphasized\naggregate fusion gains over unimodal baselines [10, 33], leaving\nsuch participant-level variation largely unexplored.\nA within-rater variance decomposition further characterizes the\noverlap between the Claude and HuBERT predictions (Table 3).\nThe two predictions are weakly correlated within raters (mean 𝑟\nbetween −.08 and +.13), and their shared 𝑅 2 is at most .04 in every\ncondition. The incremental associations are comparable in both\ndirections, indicating mutual rather than one-sided association.\nThe low shared 𝑅 2 and comparable bidirectional increments are\n\n\fSpeech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nTable 2: Pairwise accuracy (PW) and fusion gains over Claude, reported overall and by rank distance, together with changes in\nTop-1 accuracy. S1/S2 denote Sessions 1/2; F2M denotes female participants rating male partners, and M2F the reverse.\nAggregate PW\n\nFusion comparison with Claude\n\nCondition Claude HuBERT Fusion Overall PW gain\nS1 F2M\nS1 M2F\nS2 F2M\nS2 M2F\n\n.549\n.518\n.579\n.545\n\n.531\n.560\n.597\n.564\n\n.603\n.543\n.628\n.594\n\n+.054\n+.025\n+.049\n+.049\n\n𝑟 rb\n\nNear PW gain Mid PW gain Far PW gain Top-1 change\n\n.594∗∗∗\n.623∗∗\n.299∗\n.374∗\n\n+.044\n+.026\n+.031\n+.044\n\n+.076\n+.039\n+.055\n+.042\n\n+.074\n+.031\n+.091\n+.050\n\n−.025\n−.007\n+.013\n+.028\n\nNote. Claude denotes Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled. Aggregate PW (left) is macro-averaged across participants; rank-distance bins (Near = 1–2, Mid = 3–5, Far\n= 6+) are micro-averaged over all within-participant pairs (rank distance is a pair-level attribute), so bins need not average to the aggregate. PW gain and Top-1 change are\ncomputed as Fusion − Claude. 𝑟 rb is the rank-biserial effect size for the Fusion vs. Claude Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Bold marks the bin with the largest PW gain per condition.\n∗ 𝑝 < .05, ∗∗ 𝑝 < .01, ∗∗∗ 𝑝 < .001 (Holm-corrected, 𝑚 = 4).\n\nTable 3: Mutual incremental within-rater variance between\nClaude and HuBERT across the four conditions.\nIncremental 𝑅 2\nCondition\nS1 F2M\nS1 M2F\nS2 F2M\nS2 M2F\n\nHuBERT beyond Claude beyond\nClaude\nHuBERT\nShared 𝑅 2\n+.17 [.13, .22]\n+.20 [.15, .26]\n+.21 [.16, .26]\n+.14 [.11, .17]\n\n+.19 [.15, .24]\n+.15 [.11, .19]\n+.24 [.19, .29]\n+.17 [.13, .21]\n\n.04\n.01\n.01\n.03\n\nNote. Claude denotes Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled. “HuBERT\nbeyond Claude” is the increase in 𝑅 2 obtained by adding the HuBERT prediction to the\nClaude prediction; “Claude beyond HuBERT” is defined symmetrically. Shared 𝑅 2 is\nthe overlap in explained variance. Values are within-rater, in-sample averages (hence\nthe increments are ≥ 0); brackets show 95% CIs based on 2,000 participant bootstraps.\n\nconsistent with limited overlap between the two predictions, but the\ndecomposition does not by itself distinguish distinct information\nfrom variance reduction under noisy prediction.\n\n6.2\n\nLate Fusion vs. Direct Multimodal Input\n\nThe auxiliary MLLM comparison examines a different route for\nincorporating speech. Under the tested mixed-mono configuration,\nGemini’s text-plus-audio input does not consistently improve on\nits matched transcript-only input, and GPT-audio-mini does not\nsurpass the strongest transcript-only baselines. By contrast, combining independently obtained Claude and HuBERT predictions\nyields a pairwise-ordering benefit in the present configuration. The\nobserved contrast between direct multimodal input and score-level\nfusion does not establish a general advantage of score-level late\nfusion over direct multimodal inference; the comparison is specific\nto the evaluated models, prompts, and audio processing.\nScore-level fusion instead provides a modular way to evaluate the speech predictor’s additional predictive value relative to\na transcript-only LLM. The selected weights place less weight on\nHuBERT in Session 1, where it is less accurate, and relatively more\nweight on it in Session 2. As transcript-only LLMs evolve, the magnitude of speech-derived complementarity remains an empirical\nquestion across models and conditions.\n\n6.3\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n\nEvaluation target and partner-level consensus. Per-participant 𝑟\nevaluates within-participant differentiation among partners but\ndoes not distinguish rater-specific partner preference from partnerlevel consensus shared across raters. To contextualize this limitation, we construct a descriptive partner-level consensus reference\nusing the mean score each partner received from other raters. The\nreference reaches per-participant 𝑟 of .36–.48, above the Claude–\nHuBERT fusion in all four conditions (Section S1.6). These groundtruth ratings are unavailable at inference, so the reference provides\ncontext rather than a deployable baseline.\nMechanism of speech-derived complementarity. Figure 2 shows\nthat fusion gains are concentrated among participants for whom\nHuBERT has higher 𝑟 , but this association is retrospective: it requires all conversations from a participant and cannot predict fusion\nbenefit in advance. Table 3 characterizes the mutual incremental\nassociations between Claude and HuBERT. Replacing HuBERT with\nthe hand-crafted openSMILE representation does not improve on\nClaude in any condition (Table S1). These analyses do not identify\nwhich acoustic information HuBERT captures. HuBERT represents\nsequential speech structure [15] and transfers well to paralinguistic\ntasks [37]. Which cues contribute—laughter, pauses, backchannels,\nor turn-taking—remains open. Future work should test whether\nparticipant-adaptive or hierarchical models can prospectively estimate speech-branch reliability.\nDirection asymmetry and conversation round. Numerical Δ𝑟 differs across rating directions and is largest in Session 2 F2M. Because\nSession 2 combines prior-encounter exposure with a longer nominal duration, this corpus cannot separate their contributions. Future\nstudies should vary these factors independently. Modeling actor\nand partner effects, interpersonal synchrony, and turn-level dynamics such as prosodic adaptation may also help explain the observed\ncondition dependence.\nCorpus, language, and inclusivity. The evidence comes from a single Japanese speed-dating corpus with 147 participants, restricted\nto opposite-sex pairs. Whether the condition-dependent complementarity we observe generalizes across languages and pair compositions or to other short, dyadic, first-encounter interactions with\nan explicit evaluative goal remains an open empirical question.\n\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\nFigure 2: Across all four conditions, participants with higher HuBERT per-participant Pearson 𝑟 show larger Claude–HuBERT\nfusion gain Δ𝑟 over Claude, although gain magnitude varies by condition. Panels show Sessions 1/2 and female-to-male\n(F2M)/male-to-female (M2F) ratings. Points are participants; solid lines and bands show OLS fits and 95% CIs, and dashed lines\nmark zero. Because HuBERT enters the fused score, this association is descriptive rather than an independent mechanism.\nModel and modality coverage. The main analysis focuses on text\nand speech, although video cues can also predict interpersonal\noutcomes in speed dating [36]. The direct-input MLLM comparison covers two models under one prompting and audio-processing\npipeline; audio-LLM capabilities are evolving rapidly [32]. We reuse\nthe transcript-only prompt, mix the headset recordings to mono,\nand supply GPT-audio-mini with compressed MP3 audio. The supervised branches likewise use HuBERT and Sentence-T5 rather\nthan exhaustively comparing alternatives such as wav2vec 2.0 [4],\nWavLM [6], or Japanese and multilingual encoders. These choices\nbound the comparison: the experiments neither establish the general performance of direct-input MLLMs nor determine which\nspeech encoder best captures Japanese paralinguistic cues. Future work should evaluate video-aware extensions, audio-specific\nprompts, speaker-separated audio, and alternative encoders.\n\n7\n\nConclusion\n\nIn Japanese speed dating, we examined whether a supervised speech\npredictor complements a transcript-only LLM in predicting how\nmuch participants reported liking each partner. The results show\nthat speech can complement transcript-only LLM prediction, but\nnot as a uniform multimodal gain. Across both conversation rounds\nand rating directions, fusing speech and transcript-only LLM predictions significantly improves pairwise ranking accuracy over the\ntranscript-only LLM alone. By contrast, gains in per-participant\nPearson 𝑟 vary across these settings, and none is significant after\ncorrection. Retrospectively, these 𝑟 gains are larger for participants\nwhose liking is more accurately predicted by the speech predictor.\nSpeech can therefore retain predictive value even when an LLM\npredicts interpersonal attraction from conversation transcripts. For\nmultimodal social prediction, the relevant question is not simply\nwhether speech helps, but how its complementarity varies across\nevaluation metrics, conversation settings, and participants.\n\nspeed-dating corpus was collected under institutional ethics approval and written informed consent, with access restricted to\ncontract-based research. The main analysis uses transcripts and\naudio; supplementary checks also use overhead video. These data\nremain identifying and sensitive. Predictors may reflect pretraining\nbiases, and findings apply only to Japanese opposite-sex speed dating. Responsible deployment requires explicit consent, bias auditing,\nhuman oversight, and validation across broader gender identities\nand cultures.3\n\nAcknowledgments\nThis work was partially supported by JSPS KAKENHI Grant Number 26K03016, JST CREST Grant Number JPMJCR2563, JST CRONOS\nGrant Number JPMJCS24K7, and AMED under Grant Number\nJP256f0137001.\n\nReferences\n[1] Irwin Altman and Dalmas A. Taylor. 1973. Social Penetration: The Development of\nInterpersonal Relationships. Holt, Rinehart and Winston, New York.\n[2] Nalini Ambady and Robert Rosenthal. 1992. Thin Slices of Expressive Behavior\nas Predictors of Interpersonal Consequences: A Meta-Analysis. Psychological\nBulletin 111, 2 (1992), 256–274. doi:10.1037/0033-2909.111.2.256\n[3] Mostafa M. Amin, Erik Cambria, and Björn W. Schuller. 2023. Can ChatGPT’s\nResponses Boost Traditional Natural Language Processing? IEEE Intelligent\nSystems 38, 5 (2023), 5–11. doi:10.1109/MIS.2023.3305861\n[4] Alexei Baevski, Yuhao Zhou, Abdelrahman Mohamed, and Michael Auli. 2020.\nwav2vec 2.0: A Framework for Self-Supervised Learning of Speech Representations. In Proc. Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS),\nH. Larochelle, M. Ranzato, R. Hadsell, M.F. Balcan, and H. Lin (Eds.), Vol. 33.\nCurran Associates, Inc., 12449–12460. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_\nfiles/paper/2020/file/92d1e1eb1cd6f9fba3227870bb6d7f07-Paper.pdf\n[5] Ellen Berscheid and Harry T. Reis. 1998. Attraction and Close Relationships. In\nThe Handbook of Social Psychology (4 ed.), Daniel T. Gilbert, Susan T. Fiske, and\nGardner Lindzey (Eds.). Vol. 2. McGraw-Hill, Boston, 193–281.\n[6] Sanyuan Chen, Chengyi Wang, Zhengyang Chen, Yu Wu, Shujie Liu, Zhuo Chen,\nJinyu Li, Naoyuki Kanda, Takuya Yoshioka, Xiong Xiao, Jian Wu, Long Zhou,\nShuo Ren, Yanmin Qian, Yao Qian, Jian Wu, Michael Zeng, Xiangzhan Yu, and\nFuru Wei. 2022. WavLM: Large-Scale Self-Supervised Pre-Training for Full Stack\nSpeech Processing. IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing 16, 6 (2022),\n1505–1518. doi:10.1109/JSTSP.2022.3188113\n\nSafe and Responsible Innovation Statement", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 2171}}}
