{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f52a5e4f3d0286a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\tpool in the utility–redundancy plane referenced in §4.3 in the main\n1201\tpaper. Across all four conditions, HuBERT occupies a favorable\n1202\tregion among the speech candidates. It combines relatively high\n1203\tutility with limited prediction correlation with Claude.\n1204\tThe thinking-enabled auxiliary Claude configuration has similar\n1205\tper-participant 𝑟 to disabled Claude in three conditions and lower 𝑟\n1206\tin Session 2 M2F; it does not systematically improve per-participant\n1207\t\n1208\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n1209\t\n1210\tKikuchi et al.\n1211\t\n1212\tFigure S1: Complementarity landscape of candidate models across the four conditions. Each point gives the mean across\n1213\tparticipants of Pearson’s 𝑟 between a candidate model’s predictions and ground-truth liking (utility, y-axis) or the prespecified\n1214\ttranscript-only LLM’s predictions (Claude; redundancy proxy, x-axis). A favorable speech complement lies upper-left (high\n1215\tutility, low prediction correlation). The selected pair—Claude for transcript-only prediction and HuBERT for supervised speech\n1216\tprediction—is highlighted. openSMILE appears only in Session 2 panels.\n1217\t𝑟 over disabled Claude. We therefore retain disabled Claude as the\n1218\tprespecified transcript-only LLM in the main analysis.\n1219\tReplacing Claude with any supervised text model lowers fusion\n1220\t𝑟 relative to the main Claude–HuBERT fusion in every condition.\n1221\tClaude’s zero-shot transcript-only prediction is therefore not interchangeable with a supervised text prediction under this fusion\n1222\tsetup. The three supervised text alternatives rank in no stable order\n1223\tacross conditions.\n1224\tReplacing HuBERT with openSMILE lowers fusion 𝑟 in Session 2\n1225\t(Δ𝑟 = −.02 to −.09, substituted fusion minus the main Claude–\n1226\tHuBERT fusion). In Session 1, this substituted fusion matches\n1227\tClaude alone (𝑟 = .248, .163), with the weight-selection procedure\n1228\tdiscounting openSMILE almost entirely. This pattern is consistent\n1229\twith openSMILE’s standalone 𝑟 being undefined on the full Session 1 rater set; see the note to Table 1 in the main paper.\n1230\tNone of the tested within-modality (text + text) ensembles consistently improves on Claude alone. The Claude–BERT text–text combination is below Claude alone in all four conditions. The SentenceT5 combination approaches the main fusion only in Session 2 F2M\n1231\t(.306 vs. .323), while it underperforms in the other three conditions.\n1232\tThe main Claude–HuBERT pairing is the only tested combination\n1233\twhose 𝑟 numerically exceeds Claude alone in every condition; the\n1234\t\n1235\ttested text–text ensembles do not reproduce this four-condition\n1236\tpattern.\n1237\t\n1238\tS1.2\n1239\t\n1240\tPer-Participant CCC for Fusion\n1241\tComponents\n1242\t\n1243\tAlthough the main paper focuses on per-participant 𝑟 as the primary\n1244\tmetric, CCC (concordance correlation coefficient) [5] is used for\n1245\tsupervised training and fusion-weight selection for the calibrationsensitive reasons described in §4.4 in the main paper. Table S2\n1246\treports the per-participant CCC for each fusion component (Claude,\n1247\tHuBERT, and their weighted combination). Fusion CCC exceeds\n1248\tClaude’s CCC in three of the four conditions, showing qualitative\n1249\talignment with the condition-level 𝑟 results.\n1250\tFusion CCC exceeds Claude’s CCC in Session 1 F2M, Session 2\n1251\tF2M, and Session 2 M2F. Here, Δ𝑟 and ΔCCC denote Fusion minus\n1252\tClaude for per-participant 𝑟 and CCC, respectively. Both changes\n1253\tare near zero in Session 1 M2F, consistent with the near-zero fusion\n1254\tgain in 𝑟 for that condition.\n1255\t\n1256\t\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n1257\t\n1258\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n1259\t\n1260\tTable S2: Per-participant CCC for Claude, HuBERT, and their\n1261\tweighted score-level late fusion.\n1262\tComponent\n1263\t\n1264\tS1 F2M\n1265\t\n1266\tS1 M2F\n1267\t\n1268\tS2 F2M\n1269\t\n1270\tS2 M2F\n1271\t\n1272\tClaude\n1273\tHuBERT\n1274\tFusion\n1275\t\n1276\t.081±.016\n1277\t.054±.024\n1278\t.101±.019\n1279\t\n1280\t.073±.016\n1281\t.057±.024\n1282\t.071±.017\n1283\t\n1284\t.067±.017\n1285\t.057±.024\n1286\t.105±.019\n1287\t\n1288\t.078±.017\n1289\t.084±.025\n1290\t.094±.023\n1291\t\n1292\tNote. Bold indicates the best CCC per column. ± values denote the standard error of\n1293\tthe mean (SE) across participants.\n1294\t\n1295\tTable S3: Per-participant 𝑟 for equal-weight versus foldselected weighted score-level late fusion of Claude and HuBERT.\n1296\tMethod\n1297\t\n1298\tS1 F2M\n1299\t\n1300\tS1 M2F\n1301\t\n1302\tS2 F2M\n1303\t\n1304\tS2 M2F\n1305\t\n1306\tClaude (reference)\n1307\tEqual-weight average (𝑤=0.5)\n1308\tWeighted average (main)\n1309\t\n1310\t.248\n1311\t.185\n1312\t.281\n1313\t\n1314\t.163\n1315\t.162\n1316\t.164\n1317\t\n1318\t.244\n1319\t.316\n1320\t.323\n1321\t\n1322\t.238\n1323\t.250\n1324\t.248\n1325\t\n1326\tNote. Weights in the fold-selected Claude–HuBERT fusion are selected per fold via a\n1327\t21-point grid search over the pooled held-out predictions of the remaining folds (§4.3\n1328\tin the main paper). Bold marks the fold-selected Claude–HuBERT fusion.\n1329\t\n1330\tS1.3\n1331\t\n1332\tEqual-Weight vs. Weighted Fusion\n1333\t\n1334\t§6.2 in the main paper examines weighted averaging as a way to\n1335\treduce the influence of a weak component rather than produce\n1336\tuniform improvement. Table S3 reports per-participant 𝑟 for the\n1337\tmain fusion under both equal-weight (𝑤 = 0.5) and fold-selected\n1338\tweighted averaging. For the primary weighted fusion, paired Δ𝑟\n1339\trelative to Claude was +.034, +.001, +.079, and +.011 for Session 1\n1340\tF2M, Session 1 M2F, Session 2 F2M, and Session 2 M2F, respectively;\n1341\tnone reached significance after Holm correction (adjusted 𝑝 = .11,\n1342\t1.00, .29, and 1.00; 𝑚 = 4).\n1343\tIn Session 2 F2M, where HuBERT has its highest individual 𝑟 ,\n1344\tequal-weight averaging already captures most of the gain (𝑟 =.316\n1345\tvs. weighted .323). In Session 1 F2M, where HuBERT is weaker,\n1346\tequal-weight averaging falls below Claude alone (.185 vs. .248),\n1347\twhile the weighted version down-weights HuBERT and recovers\n1348\tan 𝑟 of .281. In both M2F conditions, equal-weight and weighted\n1349\taveraging are essentially tied. These results suggest that the main\n1350\trole of weighted averaging is robustness to a weak component\n1351\trather than a uniform additive gain.\n1352\tFigure S2 shows the mean selected weights across the 25 crossvalidation folds. The selection procedure assigns HuBERT comparable weight to the Claude branch in Session 2, where HuBERT has\n1353\thigher individual 𝑟 than in Session 1. In Session 1, where HuBERT\n1354\thas lower individual 𝑟 than in Session 2, the selection procedure\n1355\tconcentrates on the Claude branch. This pattern is consistent with\n1356\tthe robustness-against-noise interpretation above.\n1357\t\n1358\tS1.4\n1359\t\n1360\tIncremental Variance Decomposition\n1361\t\n1362\t§6.1 in the main paper reports positive in-sample incremental 𝑅 2\n1363\tfor each predictor given the other in all four conditions. Table S4\n1364\tprovides the full decomposition. For each participant with ≥ 3\n1365\trated partners, we compute the within-rater Pearson correlations\n1366\tbetween Claude prediction and ground truth (𝑟𝐶 ), HuBERT and\n1367\t\n1368\tFigure S2: Mean selected weights for the Claude transcriptonly and HuBERT speech predictions across the 25 crossvalidation folds, by condition.\n1369\tTable S4: Full within-rater variance decomposition of predictions from Claude and HuBERT.\n1370\tMetric\n1371\t\n137