orrelations 𝑟 (𝑦ˆ𝐻 , 𝑦 | 𝑦ˆ𝐶 ) and 𝑟 (𝑦ˆ𝐶 , 𝑦 | 𝑦ˆ𝐻 ), aggregated by Fisher-𝑧 mean. The two incremental 𝑅 2 contrasts are\n1506\treported with 95% participant-bootstrap confidence intervals (CIs;\n1507\t2,000 resamples).\n1508\tThe shared 𝑅 2 between Claude and HuBERT predictions is at\n1509\tmost .04 across the four conditions, small relative to each predictor’s\n1510\tmarginal 𝑅 2 ∈ [.17, .24]. The two increments are comparable in\n1511\tmagnitude, and neither direction is uniformly larger: Δ𝑅𝐶2 |𝐻 exceeds\n1512\tΔ𝑅𝐻2 |𝐶 in three of four conditions, with Session 1 M2F reversed.\n1513\tThis pattern is consistent with limited overlap between the two predictions. However, these increments are in-sample and nonnegative\n1514\t\n1515\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n1516\t\n1517\tKikuchi et al.\n1518\t\n1519\tTable S5: Per-participant 𝑟 versus global 𝑟 for Claude, HuBERT, and their fusion, by condition.\n1520\tCondition\n1521\t\n1522\tMetric\n1523\t\n1524\tClaude\n1525\t\n1526\tHuBERT\n1527\t\n1528\tFusion\n1529\t\n1530\tS1 F2M\n1531\t\n1532\tPer-participant 𝑟\n1533\tGlobal 𝑟\n1534\t\n1535\t.248\n1536\t.241\n1537\t\n1538\t.105\n1539\t−.043\n1540\t\n1541\t.281\n1542\t.180\n1543\t\n1544\tS1 M2F\n1545\t\n1546\tPer-participant 𝑟\n1547\tGlobal 𝑟\n1548\t\n1549\t.163\n1550\t.114\n1551\t\n1552\t.092\n1553\t.112\n1554\t\n1555\t.164\n1556\t.126\n1557\t\n1558\tS2 F2M\n1559\t\n1560\tPer-participant 𝑟\n1561\tGlobal 𝑟\n1562\t\n1563\t.244\n1564\t.238\n1565\t\n1566\t.212\n1567\t.024\n1568\t\n1569\t.323\n1570\t.134\n1571\t\n1572\tS2 M2F\n1573\t\n1574\tPer-participant 𝑟\n1575\tGlobal 𝑟\n1576\t\n1577\t.238\n1578\t.099\n1579\t\n1580\t.183\n1581\t.154\n1582\t\n1583\t.248\n1584\t.170\n1585\t\n1586\tby construction, so their mere positivity does not demonstrate outof-sample incremental value. Within-rater 𝑧-scoring leaves these\n1587\tquantities unchanged, so prediction scale compression does not\n1588\taffect the decomposition. Moreover, weak overlap between the\n1589\tpredictors cannot distinguish genuinely distinct information from\n1590\tpartially independent noise around the same underlying signal. The\n1591\tdecomposition therefore describes limited overlap and comparable\n1592\tbidirectional increments in this sample, but does not establish their\n1593\tmechanism or generalizability.\n1594\t\n1595\tS1.5\n1596\t\n1597\tPer-Participant vs. Global 𝑟\n1598\t\n1599\tPer-participant 𝑟 is adopted as the primary metric in §4.4 in the\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "21cd270d-96de-45df-8da9-189571e3db27", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:51:26.686Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.23037v1.txt", "content": "pool in the utility–redundancy plane referenced in §4.3 in the main\npaper. Across all four conditions, HuBERT occupies a favorable\nregion among the speech candidates. It combines relatively high\nutility with limited prediction correlation with Claude.\nThe thinking-enabled auxiliary Claude configuration has similar\nper-participant 𝑟 to disabled Claude in three conditions and lower 𝑟\nin Session 2 M2F; it does not systematically improve per-participant\n\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\nFigure S1: Complementarity landscape of candidate models across the four conditions. Each point gives the mean across\nparticipants of Pearson’s 𝑟 between a candidate model’s predictions and ground-truth liking (utility, y-axis) or the prespecified\ntranscript-only LLM’s predictions (Claude; redundancy proxy, x-axis). A favorable speech complement lies upper-left (high\nutility, low prediction correlation). The selected pair—Claude for transcript-only prediction and HuBERT for supervised speech\nprediction—is highlighted. openSMILE appears only in Session 2 panels.\n𝑟 over disabled Claude. We therefore retain disabled Claude as the\nprespecified transcript-only LLM in the main analysis.\nReplacing Claude with any supervised text model lowers fusion\n𝑟 relative to the main Claude–HuBERT fusion in every condition.\nClaude’s zero-shot transcript-only prediction is therefore not interchangeable with a supervised text prediction under this fusion\nsetup. The three supervised text alternatives rank in no stable order\nacross conditions.\nReplacing HuBERT with openSMILE lowers fusion 𝑟 in Session 2\n(Δ𝑟 = −.02 to −.09, substituted fusion minus the main Claude–\nHuBERT fusion). In Session 1, this substituted fusion matches\nClaude alone (𝑟 = .248, .163), with the weight-selection procedure\ndiscounting openSMILE almost entirely. This pattern is consistent\nwith openSMILE’s standalone 𝑟 being undefined on the full Session 1 rater set; see the note to Table 1 in the main paper.\nNone of the tested within-modality (text + text) ensembles consistently improves on Claude alone. The Claude–BERT text–text combination is below Claude alone in all four conditions. The SentenceT5 combination approaches the main fusion only in Session 2 F2M\n(.306 vs. .323), while it underperforms in the other three conditions.\nThe main Claude–HuBERT pairing is the only tested combination\nwhose 𝑟 numerically exceeds Claude alone in every condition; the\n\ntested text–text ensembles do not reproduce this four-condition\npattern.\n\nS1.2\n\nPer-Participant CCC for Fusion\nComponents\n\nAlthough the main paper focuses on per-participant 𝑟 as the primary\nmetric, CCC (concordance correlation coefficient) [5] is used for\nsupervised training and fusion-weight selection for the calibrationsensitive reasons described in §4.4 in the main paper. Table S2\nreports the per-participant CCC for each fusion component (Claude,\nHuBERT, and their weighted combination). Fusion CCC exceeds\nClaude’s CCC in three of the four conditions, showing qualitative\nalignment with the condition-level 𝑟 results.\nFusion CCC exceeds Claude’s CCC in Session 1 F2M, Session 2\nF2M, and Session 2 M2F. Here, Δ𝑟 and ΔCCC denote Fusion minus\nClaude for per-participant 𝑟 and CCC, respectively. Both changes\nare near zero in Session 1 M2F, consistent with the near-zero fusion\ngain in 𝑟 for that condition.\n\n\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nTable S2: Per-participant CCC for Claude, HuBERT, and their\nweighted score-level late fusion.\nComponent\n\nS1 F2M\n\nS1 M2F\n\nS2 F2M\n\nS2 M2F\n\nClaude\nHuBERT\nFusion\n\n.081±.016\n.054±.024\n.101±.019\n\n.073±.016\n.057±.024\n.071±.017\n\n.067±.017\n.057±.024\n.105±.019\n\n.078±.017\n.084±.025\n.094±.023\n\nNote. Bold indicates the best CCC per column. ± values denote the standard error of\nthe mean (SE) across participants.\n\nTable S3: Per-participant 𝑟 for equal-weight versus foldselected weighted score-level late fusion of Claude and HuBERT.\nMethod\n\nS1 F2M\n\nS1 M2F\n\nS2 F2M\n\nS2 M2F\n\nClaude (reference)\nEqual-weight average (𝑤=0.5)\nWeighted average (main)\n\n.248\n.185\n.281\n\n.163\n.162\n.164\n\n.244\n.316\n.323\n\n.238\n.250\n.248\n\nNote. Weights in the fold-selected Claude–HuBERT fusion are selected per fold via a\n21-point grid search over the pooled held-out predictions of the remaining folds (§4.3\nin the main paper). Bold marks the fold-selected Claude–HuBERT fusion.\n\nS1.3\n\nEqual-Weight vs. Weighted Fusion\n\n§6.2 in the main paper examines weighted averaging as a way to\nreduce the influence of a weak component rather than produce\nuniform improvement. Table S3 reports per-participant 𝑟 for the\nmain fusion under both equal-weight (𝑤 = 0.5) and fold-selected\nweighted averaging. For the primary weighted fusion, paired Δ𝑟\nrelative to Claude was +.034, +.001, +.079, and +.011 for Session 1\nF2M, Session 1 M2F, Session 2 F2M, and Session 2 M2F, respectively;\nnone reached significance after Holm correction (adjusted 𝑝 = .11,\n1.00, .29, and 1.00; 𝑚 = 4).\nIn Session 2 F2M, where HuBERT has its highest individual 𝑟 ,\nequal-weight averaging already captures most of the gain (𝑟 =.316\nvs. weighted .323). In Session 1 F2M, where HuBERT is weaker,\nequal-weight averaging falls below Claude alone (.185 vs. .248),\nwhile the weighted version down-weights HuBERT and recovers\nan 𝑟 of .281. In both M2F conditions, equal-weight and weighted\naveraging are essentially tied. These results suggest that the main\nrole of weighted averaging is robustness to a weak component\nrather than a uniform additive gain.\nFigure S2 shows the mean selected weights across the 25 crossvalidation folds. The selection procedure assigns HuBERT comparable weight to the Claude branch in Session 2, where HuBERT has\nhigher individual 𝑟 than in Session 1. In Session 1, where HuBERT\nhas lower individual 𝑟 than in Session 2, the selection procedure\nconcentrates on the Claude branch. This pattern is consistent with\nthe robustness-against-noise interpretation above.\n\nS1.4\n\nIncremental Variance Decomposition\n\n§6.1 in the main paper reports positive in-sample incremental 𝑅 2\nfor each predictor given the other in all four conditions. Table S4\nprovides the full decomposition. For each participant with ≥ 3\nrated partners, we compute the within-rater Pearson correlations\nbetween Claude prediction and ground truth (𝑟𝐶 ), HuBERT and\n\nFigure S2: Mean selected weights for the Claude transcriptonly and HuBERT speech predictions across the 25 crossvalidation folds, by condition.\nTable S4: Full within-rater variance decomposition of predictions from Claude and HuBERT.\nMetric\n\nS1 F2M S1 M2F S2 F2M S2 M2F\n\n𝑛 raters\n\n73\n\n72\n\n75\n\n72\n\n𝑟𝐶\n𝑟𝐻\n𝑟𝐶𝐻\n\n+.30\n+