{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b576507858714860", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1600\tmain paper. Table S5 complements the main results with the corresponding global 𝑟 (Pearson correlation over all pooled pairs) to\n1601\tshow where the two metrics diverge.\n1602\tIn Session 2 F2M, per-participant 𝑟 for the fusion (.323) exceeds\n1603\tClaude alone (.244), whereas global 𝑟 reverses this ordering (fusion .134 vs. Claude .238). The reversal shows that pooled and\n1604\twithin-participant correlations can yield different model orderings. Per-participant 𝑟 measures relative partner differentiation\n1605\twithin each rater. Computing 𝑟 separately for each rater prevents\n1606\tbetween-participant differences in score location and scale from\n1607\tcontributing to the metric. By contrast, global 𝑟 mixes within- and\n1608\tbetween-participant covariation. The table alone does not identify which between-participant component produces the reversal.\n1609\tPer-participant 𝑟 is aligned with the matchmaking task (§3.3 in the\n1610\tmain paper) and is used as the primary metric for that reason. The\n1611\treversal illustrates the consequence of the metric choice rather than\n1612\testablishing a fusion mechanism.\n1613\t\n1614\tS1.6\n1615\t\n1616\tPartner-Level Consensus in the Evaluation\n1617\tTarget\n1618\t\n1619\tPer-participant 𝑟 does not distinguish rater-specific preference from\n1620\tpartners’ generally shared appeal. To quantify the latter component\n1621\tin the evaluation target, we construct a descriptive partner-level\n1622\tconsensus reference. For each (rater, partner) pair within a session\n1623\tand conversation group, the reference prediction is the mean liking score that the same partner received from the other raters in\n1624\tthat group (leave-one-out; three or four donor ratings per pair in\n1625\tthe analyzed set). We then compute Pearson 𝑟 within each rater\n1626\tand average across raters, as in the primary evaluation. Because it\n1627\t\n1628\tTable S6: Partner-level consensus reference versus the Claude\n1629\ttranscript-only prediction, HuBERT speech prediction, and\n1630\ttheir fusion. Per-participant 𝑟 .\n1631\tΔ𝑟\n1632\tCondition Consensus ref. Claude HuBERT Fusion (Fusion − Ref.)\n1633\tS1 F2M\n1634\tS1 M2F\n1635\tS2 F2M\n1636\tS2 M2F\n1637\t\n1638\t.462\n1639\t.362\n1640\t.480\n1641\t.384\n1642\t\n1643\t.248\n1644\t.163\n1645\t.244\n1646\t.238\n1647\t\n1648\t.105\n1649\t.092\n1650\t.212\n1651\t.183\n1652\t\n1653\t.281\n1654\t.164\n1655\t.323\n1656\t.248\n1657\t\n1658\t−.180\n1659\t−.198\n1660\t−.157\n1661\t−.135\n1662\t\n1663\tMean\n1664\t\n1665\t.422\n1666\t\n1667\t.223\n1668\t\n1669\t.148\n1670\t\n1671\t.254\n1672\t\n1673\t−.168\n1674\t\n1675\tNote. The reference uses other raters’ ground-truth scores from the same session and\n1676\tconversation group; these labels are unavailable to a model at inference. Claude\n1677\tvalues use Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled, and Fusion denotes its\n1678\tweighted score-level late fusion with HuBERT. Δ𝑟 (Fusion − Ref.) is the Fusion value\n1679\tminus the consensus-reference value, computed before rounding. Model values are\n1680\treproduced from Table 1 in the main paper. Mean is the arithmetic mean of the four\n1681\tconditions.\n1682\t\n1683\tuses other raters’ outcome labels, this reference is unavailable at\n1684\tinference and serves as a diagnostic of the evaluation target, not\n1685\tas a deployable model baseline. Table S6 compares the reference\n1686\twith the main-fusion components to place their values on the same\n1687\tscale.\n1688\tThe reference exceeds the fusion in every condition, by .135–.198\n1689\tin per-participant 𝑟 . This gap is not evidence that the reference is a\n1690\tbetter predictive model, because the comparison is not informationmatched: the reference directly accesses other raters’ outcome labels, whereas the models receive conversation data. Instead, the\n1691\tresult indicates that within-rater partner ordering contains a substantial group-level consensus component in received liking; the\n1692\tpresent evaluation does not isolate uniquely rater-specific matching.\n1693\t\n1694\tS1.7\n1695\t\n1696\tFusion Robustness to LLM Choice: Claude\n1697\tvs. GPT-5.4\n1698\t\n1699\tThe main fusion (§4.3 in the main paper) uses Claude as the prespecified transcript-only LLM. GPT-5.4 is the other transcript-only\n1700\tLLM showing competitive per-participant 𝑟 in Session 1 (Table 1 in\n1701\tthe main paper). To verify that the condition-dependent Δ𝑟 pattern\n1702\tis not driven by the specific LLM, Table S7 compares the corresponding HuBERT fusions. The evaluation protocol, hyperparameter settings, and evaluated participant set are held fixed; fusion\n1703\tweights are selected separately for each underlying LLM using the\n1704\tsame procedure.\n1705\tThe condition ranking of fusion 𝑟 matches between the two\n1706\ttranscript-only LLMs (Session 2 F2M highest, Session 1 M2F lowest),\n1707\tand the per-condition differences are small relative to the reported\n1708\tstandard errors. The Δ𝑟 pattern also replicates descriptively: both\n1709\tLLMs yield the largest gain in Session 2 F2M and a null gain in\n1710\tSession 1 M2F. The Session 2 F2M Δ𝑟 is larger for GPT-5.4 (+.114)\n1711\tthan for Claude (+.079), consistent with HuBERT having more room\n1712\tto contribute when the paired LLM’s own 𝑟 is lower (.196 vs. .244).\n1713\tThus, the condition-level pattern is reproduced with one alternative\n1714\tLLM and is not unique to the Claude configuration, although this\n1715\tcomparison does not establish model-independent generality.\n1716\t\n1717\t\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n1718\t\n1719\tTable S7: Fusion robustness to transcript-only LLM choice,\n1720\twith each LLM paired with HuBERT.\n1721\tFusion 𝑟\n1722\t\n1723\tΔ𝑟 (Fusion − LLM)\n1724\t\n1725\tCondition\n1726\t\n1727\tClaude\n1728\t\n1729\tGPT-5.4\n1730\t\n1731\tClaude\n1732\t\n1733\tGPT-5.4\n1734\t\n1735\tS1 F2M\n1736\tS1 M2F\n1737\tS2 F2M\n1738\tS2 M2F\n1739\t\n1740\t.281±.049\n1741\t.164±.046\n1742\t.323±.044\n1743\t.248±.044\n1744\t\n1745\t.295±.047\n1746\t.203±.052\n1747\t.309±.049\n1748\t.255±.044\n1749\t\n1750\t+.034±.027\n1751\t+.001±.010\n1752\t+.079±.053\n1753\t+.011±.042\n1754\t\n1755\t+.039±.027\n1756\t.000±.016\n1757\t+.114±.050\n1758\t+.017±.043\n1759\t\n1760\tNote. Δ𝑟 is the paired per-participant difference between Fusion and the underlying\n1761\tLLM. ±: standard error of the mean across participants. Bold marks the largest value\n1762\tper column.\n1763\t\n1764\tS1.8\n1765\t\n1766\tLate vs. Early Fusion: Architectural\n1767\tComparison\n1768\t\n1769\tBecause the main Claude–HuBERT pair can only be fused at score\n1770\tlevel, the supervised Sentence-T5 + HuBERT pair provides the only\n1771\tsetting in which we can compare score-level late fusion with an\n1772\tutterance-level early-fusion alternative. Table 1 in the main paper\n1773\treports both variants for this supervised text–speech pair. Late\n1774\tfusion yields nearly the same 𝑟 as early fusion in Session 1 F2M\n1775\t(.103 vs. .106) and has higher 𝑟 in the other three conditions. The\n1776\tweighted score-level late fusion assigns 𝑤 T5 ≈ 0 in nearly all folds,\n1777\teffectively discarding the weak Sentence-T5 branch through an\n1778\texplicit branch-level scalar weight. The early-fusion projection\n1779\tcan in principle attenuate text dimensions, but it has no directly\n1780\tequivalent scalar gate because the 768-dimensional text channel\n1781\tis concatenated with HuBERT at every utterance. The observed\n1782\tcomparison does not by itself identify why early fusion has lower\n1783\t𝑟 in three conditions.\n1784\tEarly-fusion architecture. Sentence-T5 (768-d) and HuBERT (1024d) are concatenated at each utterance into a 1,792-dimensional\n1785\tstream. The stream is fed to a shared attention pooling + regression head, trained under the same settings as the single-branch\n1786\tbaselines.\n1787\t\n1788\tS1.9\n1789\t\n1790\tExternal Human Annotation Check\n1791\t\n1792\tTo illustrate the distinction between rank consistency and absolute\n1793\tscore agreement described in §4.4 in the main paper, three external\n1794\tannotators independently viewed 50 overhead video recordings\n1795\twith synchronized audio (25 conversations from each session) and\n1796\testimated the post-conversation liking scores in both directions. For\n1797\teach conversation–direction case, each annotator completed the\n1798\tsame 13 items used for the prediction target on the 1–9 scale. The\n1799\tannotated subset comprised cross-validation fold 17 in Session 1\n1800\tand fold 15 in Session 2, yielding 100 conversation–direction cases;\n1801\tit was not used for model training or model selection.\n1802\tAgreement on the sum of the 13 items, as measured by the\n1803\tintraclass correlation coefficient (ICC), was low for absolute agreement, ICC(2,𝑘) = .29 (95% CI [−.06, .57]), but higher for consistency,\n1804\tICC(3,𝑘) = .69 (95% CI [.57, .78]). Pairwise Pearson correlations between annotators ranged from .53 to .61. This pattern is consistent\n1805\twith substantial differences in scale placement alongside moderate\n1806\t\n1807\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n1808\t\n1809\tagreement in relative ordering, illustrating why the main evaluation reports both a relative-differentiation primary metric and a\n1810\tcalibration-sensitive secondary metric. Given the small diagnostic\n1811\tsubset, we do not treat these annotations as a human-performance\n1812\tbenchmark.\n1813\t\n1814\tS2 Evaluation and Implementation Details\n1815\tS2.1 Corpus Subset and Recording Details\n1816\tBecause sample composition and session setup affect the interpretation of the condition-level results, this section documents dataset\n1817\tdetails that are summarized more briefly in §3.1 and §3.2 of the\n1818\tmain paper. It reports condition-level liking-score descriptives, explains why each session contains 624 rather than 625 conversations,\n1819\tand clarifies how the nominal session durations were handled. Percondition means and standard deviations, computed after applying\n1820\tthe rater exclusion rule, are: Session 1 F2M 𝑀 = 4.36 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.50),\n1821\tM2F 𝑀 = 4.77 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.47); Session 2 F2M 𝑀 = 4.49 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.53),\n1822\tM2F 𝑀 = 4.94 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.47).\n1823\tConversation count. Each session contains 624 rather than the\n1824\tnominal 25 × 25 = 625 pairwise conversations: one male–female\n1825\tpair has two recorded conversations in each session, and only the\n1826\tearlier recording (by timestamp) is retained for that pair.\n1827\tSession duration. We use the session-specific recordings as provided, with nominal durations of 5 minutes for Session 1 and 10\n1828\tminutes for Session 2. Neither transcript-window construction for\n1829\tthe LLMs nor supervised preprocessing applies an additional hard\n1830\ttimestamp cutoff.\n1831\t\n1832\tS2.2\n1833\t\n1834\tCross-Validation Protocol Details\n1835\t\n1836\tBecause participants can appear in more than one conversation\n1837\tgroup, the handling of participant identities and outcome labels\n1838\tacross training, validation, and model selection is central to assessing leakage control and the scope of participant separation. §4.4\n1839\tin the main paper summarizes the 25-fold Leave-One-Group-Out\n1840\t(LOGO) protocol; this section specifies validation-fold selection and\n1841\ttraining-side exclusions, while Section S2.3 separately examines\n1842\tparticipant overlap in fusion-weight selection.\n1843\tValidation fold. For each test fold, one fold that shares no participants with the test fold is randomly selected as the validation\n1844\tfold using the fixed random seed 42. The validation fold is used for\n1845\tearly stopping (patience 20 epochs) and does not contribute to the\n1846\tfinal test metrics.\n1847\tTraining-side exclusions. From the training set we further exclude (a) participants with fewer than three partners in the given\n1848\trating direction, and (b) low-variance participants whose likingscore SD falls below the median participant SD minus 2.5 times its\n1849\tmedian absolute deviation (MAD). These exclusions avoid unstable\n1850\tparticipant-level CCC estimates from participants with few partners or near-constant ratings. They apply only to training; the test\n1851\tand validation evaluation sets are unchanged.\n1852\tLate fusion weight optimization. As described in §4.3 in the main\n1853\tpaper, the fusion weight 𝑤 is selected per test fold on the pooled\n1854\t\n1855\t\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n1856\t\n1857\tKikuchi et al.\n1858\t\n1859\tTable S8: Claude–HuBERT fusion results under pooled versus\n1860\tstrictly participant-disjoint weight selection. ΔPW denotes\n1861\tFusion − Claude, and Coupling 𝜌 denotes the Spearman correlation between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and fusion gain\n1862\tΔ𝑟 .\n1863\tFusion 𝑟\n1864\t\n1865\tΔPW (vs. Claude)\n1866\t\n1867\tCoupling 𝜌\n1868\t\n1869\tCondition\n1870\t\n1871\tPooled\n1872\t\n1873\tDisjoint\n1874\t\n1875\tPooled\n1876\t\n1877\tDisjoint\n1878\t\n1879\tPooled\n1880\t\n1881\tDisjoint\n1882\t\n1883\tS1 F2M\n1884\tS1 M2F\n1885\tS2 F2M\n1886\tS2 M2F\n1887\t\n1888\t.281\n1889\t.164\n1890\t.323\n1891\t.248\n1892\t\n1893\t.262\n1894\t.139\n1895\t.321\n1896\t.250\n1897\t\n1898\t+.054∗∗∗\n1899\t+.025∗∗\n1900\t+.049∗\n1901\t+.049∗\n1902\t\n1903\t+.046∗∗\n1904\t+.017∗\n1905\t+.050∗\n1906\t+.052∗\n1907\t\n1908\t.76\n1909\t.70\n1910\t.80\n1911\t.72\n1912\t\n1913\t.73\n1914\t.70\n1915\t.80\n1916\t.70\n1917\t\n1918\tNote. Pooled = weight selection on the pooled held-out predictions of the remaining\n1919\tfolds (the protocol used throughout the paper); Disjoint = pairs involving test-fold\n1920\tparticipants additionally removed from the selection pool. ΔPW is the aggregate\n1921\tpairwise-accuracy gain of the fusion over Claude alone; Coupling 𝜌 is the Spearman\n1922\tcorrelation between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and the per-participant fusion gain\n1923\tΔ𝑟 . ∗ 𝑝 < .05, ∗∗ 𝑝 < .01, ∗∗∗ 𝑝 < .001 (Wilcoxon, Holm-corrected, 𝑚 = 4).\n1924\t\n1925\theld-out predictions of the remaining folds, and the selected 𝑤 is\n1926\tapplied to the held-out test fold without further tuning.\n1927\t\n1928\tS2.3\n1929\t\n1930\tSensitivity to Participant Overlap in\n1931\tFusion-Weight Selection\n1932\t\n1933\tThe fusion weight 𝑤 is selected per test fold on the pooled held-out\n1934\tpredictions of the remaining folds (§4.3 in the main paper). Because\n1935\tparticipants can appear in more than one conversation group, pairs\n1936\tinvolving test-fold participants make up approximately 9% of this\n1937\tselection pool, so the pool is not strictly participant-disjoint from\n1938\tthe test fold. To quantify the effect of this coupling, we repeated the\n1939\tweight selection after removing every pair that involves a test-fold\n1940\tparticipant in either role (leaving 91% of the pool) and re-derived\n1941\tall fusion results.\n1942\tTable S8 compares the two protocols. All conclusions reported in\n1943\tthe main paper hold under both. Pairwise accuracy (PW) improves\n1944\tover Claude alone in all four conditions (Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05).\n1945\tThe fused model exceeds HuBERT alone in PW in the same three\n1946\tconditions, with Session 1 M2F remaining the exception. The Spearman association between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and fusion\n1947\tgain Δ𝑟 stays in the .70–.80 range, with its largest value in Session 2\n1948\tF2M, and no paired Δ𝑟 reaches Holm-corrected significance. The\n1949\tvisible effect is confined to fusion 𝑟 in Session 1 (from .281 to .262\n1950\tin F2M and from .164 to .139 in M2F). In these Session 1 conditions,\n1951\tHuBERT is weak, the selected weight concentrates on the Claude\n1952\tbranch, and the observed change is consistent with greater sensitivity to the composition of the selection pool. Session 2 values are\n1953\tessentially unchanged.\n1954\t\n1955\tS2.4\n1956\t\n1957\tLLM Prompt Details\n1958\t\n1959\tBecause transcript-only LLM predictions depend on input scope,\n1960\trater role, and output constraints, this section documents what\n1961\tinformation each model received and how the 13 item scores were\n1962\telicited. §4.2 in the main paper summarizes the prompt design. The\n1963\tsystem and user prompts are documented below, followed by an\n1964\tEnglish translation and the placeholder substitutions. The released\n1965\tcode contains the exact executable Japanese templates, including\n1966\t\n1967\tthe full Q1–Q13 wording; the item list is marked as omitted in the\n1968\tabridged display below. These items are the Japanese translation\n1969\tof Rubin’s Liking Scale [4]; see §3.2 in the main paper. The primary Claude configuration uses temperature 0 and forced tool use\n1970\treturning thirteen integer scores via a JSON schema.\n1971\tSystem prompt (abridged original Japanese).\n1972\tあなたはスピードデーティングの実験参加者をシミュ\n1973\tレートしています。\n1974\t状況: あなたは{evaluator_role}参加者として、{target_role}の\n1975\t相手と{duration}分間の対話をしたところです。以下\n1976\tにその対話の書き起こしが提示されます。\n1977\tタスク: 対話の書き起こしの内容のみに基づいて、相\n1978\t手の{target_role}に対するあなたの印象を、以下の13の\n1979\t質問それぞれについて1〜9の整数で回答してくださ\n1980\tい。\n1981\t回答尺度: 1 = 全くそう思わない; 2 = そう思わない; 3 =\n1982\tあまりそう思わない; 4 = どちらかといえばそう思わな\n1983\tい; 5 = どちらでもない; 6 = どちらかといえばそう思う;\n1984\t7 = ややそう思う; 8 = そう思う; 9 = 非常にそう思う。\n1985\t[Q1–Q13 omitted from this abridged display.]\n1986\tすべての質問に必ず回答してください。対話の内容か\n1987\tら判断できない場合は、対話の雰囲気や文脈から最も\n1988\t妥当と思われるスコアをつけてください。\n1989\t\n1990\tUser prompt (original Japanese).\n1991\t以下は、あなた（{evaluator_role}）と相手（{target_role}）\n1992\tの対話の書き起こしです。\n1993\t{transcript}\n1994\t上記の対話に基づいて、13の質問項目すべてに回答し\n1995\tてください。\n1996\t\n1997\tSystem prompt (abridged English translation).\n1998\tYou are simulating a speed-dating study participant.\n1999\tContext: You have just completed a {duration}-minute conversation with a {target_role} partner as the {evaluator_role}\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "4928fc3c-9506-4ad8-b321-83bf17b95e9b", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:51:38.345Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.23037v1.txt", "content": "main paper. Table S5 complements the main results with the corresponding global 𝑟 (Pearson correlation over all pooled pairs) to\nshow where the two metrics diverge.\nIn Session 2 F2M, per-participant 𝑟 for the fusion (.323) exceeds\nClaude alone (.244), whereas global 𝑟 reverses this ordering (fusion .134 vs. Claude .238). The reversal shows that pooled and\nwithin-participant correlations can yield different model orderings. Per-participant 𝑟 measures relative partner differentiation\nwithin each rater. Computing 𝑟 separately for each rater prevents\nbetween-participant differences in score location and scale from\ncontributing to the metric. By contrast, global 𝑟 mixes within- and\nbetween-participant covariation. The table alone does not identify which between-participant component produces the reversal.\nPer-participant 𝑟 is aligned with the matchmaking task (§3.3 in the\nmain paper) and is used as the primary metric for that reason. The\nreversal illustrates the consequence of the metric choice rather than\nestablishing a fusion mechanism.\n\nS1.6\n\nPartner-Level Consensus in the Evaluation\nTarget\n\nPer-participant 𝑟 does not distinguish rater-specific preference from\npartners’ generally shared appeal. To quantify the latter component\nin the evaluation target, we construct a descriptive partner-level\nconsensus reference. For each (rater, partner) pair within a session\nand conversation group, the reference prediction is the mean liking score that the same partner received from the other raters in\nthat group (leave-one-out; three or four donor ratings per pair in\nthe analyzed set). We then compute Pearson 𝑟 within each rater\nand average across raters, as in the primary evaluation. Because it\n\nTable S6: Partner-level consensus reference versus the Claude\ntranscript-only prediction, HuBERT speech prediction, and\ntheir fusion. Per-participant 𝑟 .\nΔ𝑟\nCondition Consensus ref. Claude HuBERT Fusion (Fusion − Ref.)\nS1 F2M\nS1 M2F\nS2 F2M\nS2 M2F\n\n.462\n.362\n.480\n.384\n\n.248\n.163\n.244\n.238\n\n.105\n.092\n.212\n.183\n\n.281\n.164\n.323\n.248\n\n−.180\n−.198\n−.157\n−.135\n\nMean\n\n.422\n\n.223\n\n.148\n\n.254\n\n−.168\n\nNote. The reference uses other raters’ ground-truth scores from the same session and\nconversation group; these labels are unavailable to a model at inference. Claude\nvalues use Claude Sonnet 4.6 with extended thinking disabled, and Fusion denotes its\nweighted score-level late fusion with HuBERT. Δ𝑟 (Fusion − Ref.) is the Fusion value\nminus the consensus-reference value, computed before rounding. Model values are\nreproduced from Table 1 in the main paper. Mean is the arithmetic mean of the four\nconditions.\n\nuses other raters’ outcome labels, this reference is unavailable at\ninference and serves as a diagnostic of the evaluation target, not\nas a deployable model baseline. Table S6 compares the reference\nwith the main-fusion components to place their values on the same\nscale.\nThe reference exceeds the fusion in every condition, by .135–.198\nin per-participant 𝑟 . This gap is not evidence that the reference is a\nbetter predictive model, because the comparison is not informationmatched: the reference directly accesses other raters’ outcome labels, whereas the models receive conversation data. Instead, the\nresult indicates that within-rater partner ordering contains a substantial group-level consensus component in received liking; the\npresent evaluation does not isolate uniquely rater-specific matching.\n\nS1.7\n\nFusion Robustness to LLM Choice: Claude\nvs. GPT-5.4\n\nThe main fusion (§4.3 in the main paper) uses Claude as the prespecified transcript-only LLM. GPT-5.4 is the other transcript-only\nLLM showing competitive per-participant 𝑟 in Session 1 (Table 1 in\nthe main paper). To verify that the condition-dependent Δ𝑟 pattern\nis not driven by the specific LLM, Table S7 compares the corresponding HuBERT fusions. The evaluation protocol, hyperparameter settings, and evaluated participant set are held fixed; fusion\nweights are selected separately for each underlying LLM using the\nsame procedure.\nThe condition ranking of fusion 𝑟 matches between the two\ntranscript-only LLMs (Session 2 F2M highest, Session 1 M2F lowest),\nand the per-condition differences are small relative to the reported\nstandard errors. The Δ𝑟 pattern also replicates descriptively: both\nLLMs yield the largest gain in Session 2 F2M and a null gain in\nSession 1 M2F. The Session 2 F2M Δ𝑟 is larger for GPT-5.4 (+.114)\nthan for Claude (+.079), consistent with HuBERT having more room\nto contribute when the paired LLM’s own 𝑟 is lower (.196 vs. .244).\nThus, the condition-level pattern is reproduced with one alternative\nLLM and is not unique to the Claude configuration, although this\ncomparison does not establish model-independent generality.\n\n\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nTable S7: Fusion robustness to transcript-only LLM choice,\nwith each LLM paired with HuBERT.\nFusion 𝑟\n\nΔ𝑟 (Fusion − LLM)\n\nCondition\n\nClaude\n\nGPT-5.4\n\nClaude\n\nGPT-5.4\n\nS1 F2M\nS1 M2F\nS2 F2M\nS2 M2F\n\n.281±.049\n.164±.046\n.323±.044\n.248±.044\n\n.295±.047\n.203±.052\n.309±.049\n.255±.044\n\n+.034±.027\n+.001±.010\n+.079±.053\n+.011±.042\n\n+.039±.027\n.000±.016\n+.114±.050\n+.017±.043\n\nNote. Δ𝑟 is the paired per-participant difference between Fusion and the underlying\nLLM. ±: standard error of the mean across participants. Bold marks the largest value\nper column.\n\nS1.8\n\nLate vs. Early Fusion: Architectural\nComparison\n\nBecause the main Claude–HuBERT pair can only be fused at score\nlevel, the supervised Sentence-T5 + HuBERT pair provides the only\nsetting in which we can compare score-level late fusion with an\nutterance-level early-fusion alternative. Table 1 in the main paper\nreports both variants for this supervised text–speech pair. Late\nfusion yields nearly the same 𝑟 as early fusion in Session 1 F2M\n(.103 vs. .106) and has higher 𝑟 in the other three conditions. The\nweighted score-level late fusion assigns 𝑤 T5 ≈ 0 in nearly all folds,\neffectively discarding the weak Sentence-T5 branch through an\nexplicit branch-level scalar weight. The early-fusion projection\ncan in principle attenuate text dimensions, but it has no directly\nequivalent scalar gate because the 768-dimensional text channel\nis concatenated with HuBERT at every utterance. The observed\ncomparison does not by itself identify why early fusion has lower\n𝑟 in three conditions.\nEarly-fusion architecture. Sentence-T5 (768-d) and HuBERT (1024d) are concatenated at each utterance into a 1,792-dimensional\nstream. The stream is fed to a shared attention pooling + regression head, trained under the same settings as the single-branch\nbaselines.\n\nS1.9\n\nExternal Human Annotation Check\n\nTo illustrate the distinction between rank consistency and absolute\nscore agreement described in §4.4 in the main paper, three external\nannotators independently viewed 50 overhead video recordings\nwith synchronized audio (25 conversations from each session) and\nestimated the post-conversation liking scores in both directions. For\neach conversation–direction case, each annotator completed the\nsame 13 items used for the prediction target on the 1–9 scale. The\nannotated subset comprised cross-validation fold 17 in Session 1\nand fold 15 in Session 2, yielding 100 conversation–direction cases;\nit was not used for model training or model selection.\nAgreement on the sum of the 13 items, as measured by the\nintraclass correlation coefficient (ICC), was low for absolute agreement, ICC(2,𝑘) = .29 (95% CI [−.06, .57]), but higher for consistency,\nICC(3,𝑘) = .69 (95% CI [.57, .78]). Pairwise Pearson correlations between annotators ranged from .53 to .61. This pattern is consistent\nwith substantial differences in scale placement alongside moderate\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nagreement in relative ordering, illustrating why the main evaluation reports both a relative-differentiation primary metric and a\ncalibration-sensitive secondary metric. Given the small diagnostic\nsubset, we do not treat these annotations as a human-performance\nbenchmark.\n\nS2 Evaluation and Implementation Details\nS2.1 Corpus Subset and Recording Details\nBecause sample composition and session setup affect the interpretation of the condition-level results, this section documents dataset\ndetails that are summarized more briefly in §3.1 and §3.2 of the\nmain paper. It reports condition-level liking-score descriptives, explains why each session contains 624 rather than 625 conversations,\nand clarifies how the nominal session durations were handled. Percondition means and standard deviations, computed after applying\nthe rater exclusion rule, are: Session 1 F2M 𝑀 = 4.36 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.50),\nM2F 𝑀 = 4.77 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.47); Session 2 F2M 𝑀 = 4.49 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.53),\nM2F 𝑀 = 4.94 (𝑆𝐷 = 1.47).\nConversation count. Each session contains 624 rather than the\nnominal 25 × 25 = 625 pairwise conversations: one male–female\npair has two recorded conversations in each session, and only the\nearlier recording (by timestamp) is retained for that pair.\nSession duration. We use the session-specific recordings as provided, with nominal durations of 5 minutes for Session 1 and 10\nminutes for Session 2. Neither transcript-window construction for\nthe LLMs nor supervised preprocessing applies an additional hard\ntimestamp cutoff.\n\nS2.2\n\nCross-Validation Protocol Details\n\nBecause participants can appear in more than one conversation\ngroup, the handling of participant identities and outcome labels\nacross training, validation, and model selection is central to assessing leakage control and the scope of participant separation. §4.4\nin the main paper summarizes the 25-fold Leave-One-Group-Out\n(LOGO) protocol; this section specifies validation-fold selection and\ntraining-side exclusions, while Section S2.3 separately examines\nparticipant overlap in fusion-weight selection.\nValidation fold. For each test fold, one fold that shares no participants with the test fold is randomly selected as the validation\nfold using the fixed random seed 42. The validation fold is used for\nearly stopping (patience 20 epochs) and does not contribute to the\nfinal test metrics.\nTraining-side exclusions. From the training set we further exclude (a) participants with fewer than three partners in the given\nrating direction, and (b) low-variance participants whose likingscore SD falls below the median participant SD minus 2.5 times its\nmedian absolute deviation (MAD). These exclusions avoid unstable\nparticipant-level CCC estimates from participants with few partners or near-constant ratings. They apply only to training; the test\nand validation evaluation sets are unchanged.\nLate fusion weight optimization. As described in §4.3 in the main\npaper, the fusion weight 𝑤 is selected per test fold on the pooled\n\n\fICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nKikuchi et al.\n\nTable S8: Claude–HuBERT fusion results under pooled versus\nstrictly participant-disjoint weight selection. ΔPW denotes\nFusion − Claude, and Coupling 𝜌 denotes the Spearman correlation between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and fusion gain\nΔ𝑟 .\nFusion 𝑟\n\nΔPW (vs. Claude)\n\nCoupling 𝜌\n\nCondition\n\nPooled\n\nDisjoint\n\nPooled\n\nDisjoint\n\nPooled\n\nDisjoint\n\nS1 F2M\nS1 M2F\nS2 F2M\nS2 M2F\n\n.281\n.164\n.323\n.248\n\n.262\n.139\n.321\n.250\n\n+.054∗∗∗\n+.025∗∗\n+.049∗\n+.049∗\n\n+.046∗∗\n+.017∗\n+.050∗\n+.052∗\n\n.76\n.70\n.80\n.72\n\n.73\n.70\n.80\n.70\n\nNote. Pooled = weight selection on the pooled held-out predictions of the remaining\nfolds (the protocol used throughout the paper); Disjoint = pairs involving test-fold\nparticipants additionally removed from the selection pool. ΔPW is the aggregate\npairwise-accuracy gain of the fusion over Claude alone; Coupling 𝜌 is the Spearman\ncorrelation between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and the per-participant fusion gain\nΔ𝑟 . ∗ 𝑝 < .05, ∗∗ 𝑝 < .01, ∗∗∗ 𝑝 < .001 (Wilcoxon, Holm-corrected, 𝑚 = 4).\n\nheld-out predictions of the remaining folds, and the selected 𝑤 is\napplied to the held-out test fold without further tuning.\n\nS2.3\n\nSensitivity to Participant Overlap in\nFusion-Weight Selection\n\nThe fusion weight 𝑤 is selected per test fold on the pooled held-out\npredictions of the remaining folds (§4.3 in the main paper). Because\nparticipants can appear in more than one conversation group, pairs\ninvolving test-fold participants make up approximately 9% of this\nselection pool, so the pool is not strictly participant-disjoint from\nthe test fold. To quantify the effect of this coupling, we repeated the\nweight selection after removing every pair that involves a test-fold\nparticipant in either role (leaving 91% of the pool) and re-derived\nall fusion results.\nTable S8 compares the two protocols. All conclusions reported in\nthe main paper hold under both. Pairwise accuracy (PW) improves\nover Claude alone in all four conditions (Holm-corrected 𝑝 < .05).\nThe fused model exceeds HuBERT alone in PW in the same three\nconditions, with Session 1 M2F remaining the exception. The Spearman association between HuBERT’s per-participant 𝑟 and fusion\ngain Δ𝑟 stays in the .70–.80 range, with its largest value in Session 2\nF2M, and no paired Δ𝑟 reaches Holm-corrected significance. The\nvisible effect is confined to fusion 𝑟 in Session 1 (from .281 to .262\nin F2M and from .164 to .139 in M2F). In these Session 1 conditions,\nHuBERT is weak, the selected weight concentrates on the Claude\nbranch, and the observed change is consistent with greater sensitivity to the composition of the selection pool. Session 2 values are\nessentially unchanged.\n\nS2.4\n\nLLM Prompt Details\n\nBecause transcript-only LLM predictions depend on input scope,\nrater role, and output constraints, this section documents what\ninformation each model received and how the 13 item scores were\nelicited. §4.2 in the main paper summarizes the prompt design. The\nsystem and user prompts are documented below, followed by an\nEnglish translation and the placeholder substitutions. The released\ncode contains the exact executable Japanese templates, including\n\nthe full Q1–Q13 wording; the item list is marked as omitted in the\nabridged display below. These items are the Japanese translation\nof Rubin’s Liking Scale [4]; see §3.2 in the main paper. The primary Claude configuration uses temperature 0 and forced tool use\nreturning thirteen integer scores via a JSON schema.\nSystem prompt (abridged original Japanese).\nあなたはスピードデーティングの実験参加者をシミュ\nレートしています。\n状況: あなたは{evaluator_role}参加者として、{target_role}の\n相手と{duration}分間の対話をしたところです。以下\nにその対話の書き起こしが提示されます。\nタスク: 対話の書き起こしの内容のみに基づいて、相\n手の{target_role}に対するあなたの印象を、以下の13の\n質問それぞれについて1〜9の整数で回答してくださ\nい。\n回答尺度: 1 = 全くそう思わない; 2 = そう思わない; 3 =\nあまりそう思わない; 4 = どちらかといえばそう思わな\nい; 5 = どちらでもない; 6 = どちらかといえばそう思う;\n7 = ややそう思う; 8 = そう思う; 9 = 非常にそう思う。\n[Q1–Q13 omitted from this abridged display.]\nすべての質問に必ず回答してください。対話の内容か\nら判断できない場合は、対話の雰囲気や文脈から最も\n妥当と思われるスコアをつけてください。\n\nUser prompt (original Japanese).\n以下は、あなた（{evaluator_role}）と相手（{target_role}）\nの対話の書き起こしです。\n{transcript}\n上記の対話に基づいて、13の質問項目すべてに回答し\nてください。\n\nSystem prompt (abridged English translation).\nYou are simulating a speed-dating study participant.\nContext: You have just completed a {duration}-minute conversation with a {target_role} partner as the {evaluator_role}", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1600, "totalLines": 2171}}}
