{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a405cb122ae4ab1a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2000\tside. The transcript of that conversation is provided below.\n2001\tTask: Based solely on the transcript, rate your impression of\n2002\tthe {target_role} partner on each of the 13 items below\n2003\tusing an integer between 1 and 9.\n2004\tRating scale: 1 = strongly disagree; 2 = disagree; 3 = somewhat disagree; 4 = mildly disagree; 5 = neither agree nor\n2005\tdisagree; 6 = mildly agree; 7 = somewhat agree; 8 = agree; 9\n2006\t= strongly agree.\n2007\t[Q1–Q13 omitted from this abridged translation.]\n2008\tPlease answer every question. If the transcript does not\n2009\tgive a clear signal, use the atmosphere and context of the\n2010\tconversation to assign the most plausible score.\n2011\t\n2012\tUser prompt (English translation).\n2013\tBelow is the transcript of your conversation ({evaluator_role})\n2014\twith your partner ({target_role}).\n2015\t{transcript}\n2016\tBased on this transcript, please answer all thirteen items.\n2017\t\n2018\tTranscript format. Lines of the form [MM:SS] 男性: utterance\n2019\tor [MM:SS] 女性: utterance (“男性” = male, “女性” = female) are\n2020\tconcatenated in chronological order for the entire session. The complete chronological transcript is provided to each LLM, including\n2021\tutterances from both the rater and the partner; unlike the supervised branches, no rater-side filtering is applied.\n2022\t\n2023\t\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n2024\t\n2025\tPlaceholder substitution.\n2026\tVariable\n2027\t\n2028\tF2M\n2029\t\n2030\tM2F\n2031\t\n2032\t{evaluator_role}\n2033\t{target_role}\n2034\t\n2035\t女性\n2036\t男性\n2037\t\n2038\t男性\n2039\t女性\n2040\t\n2041\tVariable\n2042\t{duration}\n2043\t\n2044\tSession 1\n2045\t\n2046\tSession 2\n2047\t\n2048\t5\n2049\t\n2050\t10\n2051\t\n2052\tFor transcript-only input, the compared LLMs use the same base\n2053\tprompt (Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Gemma 3\n2054\t12B-IT), with the output format adapted to each API’s JSON schema,\n2055\ttool, or structured-output mechanism. For text-plus-audio input,\n2056\twe use Gemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audio-mini; the input-description\n2057\tsentence and task instruction additionally refer to the supplied\n2058\taudio. The exact variants are included in the released code.\n2059\t\n2060\tS2.5\n2061\t\n2062\topenSMILE Feature Specification\n2063\t\n2064\topenSMILE appears as a substitution baseline for the speech branch,\n2065\tso the feature inventory is included to define what information that\n2066\tbaseline represents. The openSMILE speech baseline in Table 1\n2067\tin the main paper and in Table S1 uses a 338-dimensional feature\n2068\tvector extracted per utterance with the openSMILE toolkit. We\n2069\tuse the emobase configuration for the functionals, restricted to\n2070\teight low-level descriptors (LLDs): F0, F0 envelope, line spectral\n2071\tpair frequencies, MFCC, PCM intensity, PCM loudness, PCM zerocrossing rate, and voicing probability. Each LLD is summarized by\n2072\tstandard statistical functionals (mean, standard deviation, range, extrema, linear-regression coefficients and error, skewness, kurtosis),\n2073\tyielding 338 features in total.\n2074\t\n2075\tS3 Exploratory Analyses and Extensions\n2076\tS3.1 Candidate Indicators of Fusion Gain\n2077\tThe coupling analysis in the main paper shows where fusion helps,\n2078\tbut not whether simple participant or linguistic descriptors identify those cases. We include this null search to test such post hoc\n2079\tindicators and to avoid over-interpreting fusion gain as evidence of\n2080\ta specific linguistic mechanism. For each rater, the mean and SD of\n2081\tthe true liking scores were correlated with fusion gain Δ𝑟 (Fusion −\n2082\tClaude) via Spearman correlation. In Session 2, SD yielded 𝜌 = −.20\n2083\t(F2M) and +.04 (M2F), while the mean yielded 𝜌 = +.09 (F2M) and\n2084\t−.05 (M2F); all Holm-corrected 𝑝 > .16, with correction applied to\n2085\tthe two descriptors within each direction (𝑚 = 2).\n2086\tIn Session 2, we also tested 69 J-LIWC categories for both the\n2087\trater and partner (138 tests per direction), applying Benjamini–\n2088\tHochberg false discovery rate (FDR) correction at 𝑞 = .05 within\n2089\teach direction. No association survived correction. A nominal association between partner personal-pronoun frequency and fusion\n2090\tgain in Session 2 F2M (𝜌 = +.29, uncorrected 𝑝 = .011) did not replicate in M2F (𝜌 = −.09). We therefore found no direction-robust candidate indicator and do not interpret these exploratory associations\n2091\tas evidence of a linguistic mechanism or statistical independence.\n2092\tThis null search is discussed in §6.1 in the main paper.\n2093\t\n2094\tS3.2\n2095\t\n2096\tVisual Modality Extension (V-JEPA 2.1)\n2097\t\n2098\tAs noted in §6.3 in the main paper, the main analysis is restricted\n2099\tto text and speech. To check whether available video qualitatively\n2100\t\n2101\tICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n2102\t\n2103\talters the reported pattern, we report a preliminary visual extension\n2104\tusing each rater’s bust-up video encoded locally with frozen VJEPA 2.1 ViT-Giant [6]; video is not sent to an external API. Frames\n2105\tare sampled at 1 fps and grouped into 64-frame clips. Average\n2106\tpooling over spatial tokens produces a 1,408-dimensional temporal\n2107\tembedding, which is passed through the same rater-side attentionpooling and regression architecture as the supervised branches in\n2108\t§4.1 of the main paper. Table S9 reports the resulting unimodal,\n2109\tbimodal, and trimodal performance.\n2110\tTable S9: Preliminary extension of the Claude–HuBERT fusion with V-JEPA 2.1. Per-participant 𝑟 .\n2111\tConfiguration\n2112\t\n2113\tS1 F2M S1 M2F S2 F2M S2 M2F\n2114\t\n2115\tV-JEPA 2.1 alone\n2116\t−.022\n2117\tClaude + HuBERT (main bimodal) .281\n2118\tClaude + HuBERT + V-JEPA 2.1\n2119\t.233\n2120\t\n2121\t.120\n2122\t.164\n2123\t.165\n2124\t\n2125\t.203\n2126\t.323\n2127\t.359\n2128\t\n2129\t−.004\n2130\t.248\n2131\t.250\n2132\t\n2133\tRelative to the main bimodal result, the trimodal result decreases\n2134\tin Session 1 F2M, increases by only .001–.002 in the two M2F conditions, and increases by .036 in Session 2 F2M. Because this branch is\n2135\tpreliminary and lies outside the speech-focused scope of the main\n2136\tanalysis, we do not interpret its condition-specific pattern further.\n2137\t\n2138\tReferences\n2139\t[1] Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, and Kristina Toutanova. 2019. BERT:\n2140\tPre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding. In\n2141\tProc. Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational\n2142\tLinguistics: Human Language Technologies (NAACL-HLT), Jill Burstein, Christy\n2143\tDoran, and Thamar Solorio (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics,\n2144\tMinneapolis, Minnesota, 4171–4186. doi:10.18653/v1/N19-1423\n2145\t[2] Florian Eyben, Martin Wöllmer, and Björn Schuller. 2010. openSMILE: The Munich\n2146\tVersatile and Fast Open-Source Audio Feature Extractor. In Proc. ACM International\n2147\tConference on Multimedia (ACM MM) (Firenze, Italy) (MM ’10). Association for\n2148\tComputing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, 1459–1462. doi:10.1145/1873951.\n2149\t1874246\n2150\t[3] Tasuku Igarashi, Shimpei Okuda, and Kazutoshi Sasahara. 2022. Development of\n2151\tthe Japanese Version of the Linguistic Inquiry and Word Count Dictionary 2015.\n2152\tFrontiers in Psychology 13 (2022), 841534. doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2022.841534\n2153\t[4] Ryo Ishii, Fumio Nihei, Yoko Ishii, Atsushi Otsuka, Kazuya Matsuo, Narichika\n2154\tNomoto, Atsushi Fukayama, and Takao Nakamura. 2023. Prediction of LoveLike Scores After Speed Dating Based on Pre-obtainable Personal Characteristic\n2155\tInformation. In Human-Computer Interaction – INTERACT 2023 (Lecture Notes in\n2156\tComputer Science, Vol. 14145), José Abdelnour Nocera, Marta Kristín Lárusdóttir,\n2157\tHelen Petrie, Antonio Piccinno, and Marco Winckler (Eds.). Springer Nature\n2158\tSwitzerland, Cham, 551–556. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-42293-5_71\n2159\t[5] Lawrence I-Kuei Lin. 1989. A Concordance Correlation Coefficient to Evaluate\n2160\tReproducibility. Biometrics 45, 1 (1989), 255–268. doi:10.2307/2532051\n2161\t[6] Lorenzo Mur-Labadia, Matthew Muckley, Amir Bar, Mido Assran, Koustuv\n2162\tSinha, Mike Rabbat, Yann LeCun, Nicolas Ballas, and Adrien Bardes. 2026.\n2163\tV-JEPA 2.1: Unlocking Dense Features in Video Self-Supervised Learning.\n2164\tarXiv:2603.14482 [cs.CV] doi:10.48550/arXiv.2603.14482\n2165\t[7] Jianmo Ni, Gustavo Hernandez Abrego, Noah Constant, Ji Ma, Keith Hall, Daniel\n2166\tCer, and Yinfei Yang. 2022. Sentence-T5: Scalable Sentence Encoders from Pretrained Text-to-Text Models. In Findings of the Association for Computational\n2167\tLinguistics: ACL 2022, Smaranda Muresan, Preslav Nakov, and Aline Villavicencio\n2168\t(Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Dublin, Ireland, 1864–1874.\n2169\tdoi:10.18653/v1/2022.findings-acl.146\n2170\t\n2171\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cd706f1c-7beb-40b6-b300-88f95f883d87", "uuid": "a1813005-d972-4074-8b7e-4f464a6d4d6e", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:52:03.323Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.23037v1.txt", "content": "side. The transcript of that conversation is provided below.\nTask: Based solely on the transcript, rate your impression of\nthe {target_role} partner on each of the 13 items below\nusing an integer between 1 and 9.\nRating scale: 1 = strongly disagree; 2 = disagree; 3 = somewhat disagree; 4 = mildly disagree; 5 = neither agree nor\ndisagree; 6 = mildly agree; 7 = somewhat agree; 8 = agree; 9\n= strongly agree.\n[Q1–Q13 omitted from this abridged translation.]\nPlease answer every question. If the transcript does not\ngive a clear signal, use the atmosphere and context of the\nconversation to assign the most plausible score.\n\nUser prompt (English translation).\nBelow is the transcript of your conversation ({evaluator_role})\nwith your partner ({target_role}).\n{transcript}\nBased on this transcript, please answer all thirteen items.\n\nTranscript format. Lines of the form [MM:SS] 男性: utterance\nor [MM:SS] 女性: utterance (“男性” = male, “女性” = female) are\nconcatenated in chronological order for the entire session. The complete chronological transcript is provided to each LLM, including\nutterances from both the rater and the partner; unlike the supervised branches, no rater-side filtering is applied.\n\n\fSupplementary Appendix to: Speech Signals Complement LLMs for Predicting Interpersonal Attraction in Speed Dating\n\nPlaceholder substitution.\nVariable\n\nF2M\n\nM2F\n\n{evaluator_role}\n{target_role}\n\n女性\n男性\n\n男性\n女性\n\nVariable\n{duration}\n\nSession 1\n\nSession 2\n\n5\n\n10\n\nFor transcript-only input, the compared LLMs use the same base\nprompt (Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Gemma 3\n12B-IT), with the output format adapted to each API’s JSON schema,\ntool, or structured-output mechanism. For text-plus-audio input,\nwe use Gemini 2.5 Flash and GPT-audio-mini; the input-description\nsentence and task instruction additionally refer to the supplied\naudio. The exact variants are included in the released code.\n\nS2.5\n\nopenSMILE Feature Specification\n\nopenSMILE appears as a substitution baseline for the speech branch,\nso the feature inventory is included to define what information that\nbaseline represents. The openSMILE speech baseline in Table 1\nin the main paper and in Table S1 uses a 338-dimensional feature\nvector extracted per utterance with the openSMILE toolkit. We\nuse the emobase configuration for the functionals, restricted to\neight low-level descriptors (LLDs): F0, F0 envelope, line spectral\npair frequencies, MFCC, PCM intensity, PCM loudness, PCM zerocrossing rate, and voicing probability. Each LLD is summarized by\nstandard statistical functionals (mean, standard deviation, range, extrema, linear-regression coefficients and error, skewness, kurtosis),\nyielding 338 features in total.\n\nS3 Exploratory Analyses and Extensions\nS3.1 Candidate Indicators of Fusion Gain\nThe coupling analysis in the main paper shows where fusion helps,\nbut not whether simple participant or linguistic descriptors identify those cases. We include this null search to test such post hoc\nindicators and to avoid over-interpreting fusion gain as evidence of\na specific linguistic mechanism. For each rater, the mean and SD of\nthe true liking scores were correlated with fusion gain Δ𝑟 (Fusion −\nClaude) via Spearman correlation. In Session 2, SD yielded 𝜌 = −.20\n(F2M) and +.04 (M2F), while the mean yielded 𝜌 = +.09 (F2M) and\n−.05 (M2F); all Holm-corrected 𝑝 > .16, with correction applied to\nthe two descriptors within each direction (𝑚 = 2).\nIn Session 2, we also tested 69 J-LIWC categories for both the\nrater and partner (138 tests per direction), applying Benjamini–\nHochberg false discovery rate (FDR) correction at 𝑞 = .05 within\neach direction. No association survived correction. A nominal association between partner personal-pronoun frequency and fusion\ngain in Session 2 F2M (𝜌 = +.29, uncorrected 𝑝 = .011) did not replicate in M2F (𝜌 = −.09). We therefore found no direction-robust candidate indicator and do not interpret these exploratory associations\nas evidence of a linguistic mechanism or statistical independence.\nThis null search is discussed in §6.1 in the main paper.\n\nS3.2\n\nVisual Modality Extension (V-JEPA 2.1)\n\nAs noted in §6.3 in the main paper, the main analysis is restricted\nto text and speech. To check whether available video qualitatively\n\nICMI ’26, October 05–09, 2026, Napoli, Italy\n\nalters the reported pattern, we report a preliminary visual extension\nusing each rater’s bust-up video encoded locally with frozen VJEPA 2.1 ViT-Giant [6]; video is not sent to an external API. Frames\nare sampled at 1 fps and grouped into 64-frame clips. Average\npooling over spatial tokens produces a 1,408-dimensional temporal\nembedding, which is passed through the same rater-side attentionpooling and regression architecture as the supervised branches in\n§4.1 of the main paper. Table S9 reports the resulting unimodal,\nbimodal, and trimodal performance.\nTable S9: Preliminary extension of the Claude–HuBERT fusion with V-JEPA 2.1. Per-participant 𝑟 .\nConfiguration\n\nS1 F2M S1 M2F S2 F2M S2 M2F\n\nV-JEPA 2.1 alone\n−.022\nClaude + HuBERT (main bimodal) .281\nClaude + HuBERT + V-JEPA 2.1\n.233\n\n.120\n.164\n.165\n\n.203\n.323\n.359\n\n−.004\n.248\n.250\n\nRelative to the main bimodal result, the trimodal result decreases\nin Session 1 F2M, increases by only .001–.002 in the two M2F conditions, and increases by .036 in Session 2 F2M. Because this branch is\npreliminary and lies outside the speech-focused scope of the main\nanalysis, we do not interpret its condition-specific pattern further.\n\nReferences\n[1] Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, and Kristina Toutanova. 2019. BERT:\nPre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding. In\nProc. Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational\nLinguistics: Human Language Technologies (NAACL-HLT), Jill Burstein, Christy\nDoran, and Thamar Solorio (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics,\nMinneapolis, Minnesota, 4171–4186. doi:10.18653/v1/N19-1423\n[2] Florian Eyben, Martin Wöllmer, and Björn Schuller. 2010. openSMILE: The Munich\nVersatile and Fast Open-Source Audio Feature Extractor. In Proc. ACM International\nConference on Multimedia (ACM MM) (Firenze, Italy) (MM ’10). Association for\nComputing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, 1459–1462. doi:10.1145/1873951.\n1874246\n[3] Tasuku Igarashi, Shimpei Okuda, and Kazutoshi Sasahara. 2022. Development of\nthe Japanese Version of the Linguistic Inquiry and Word Count Dictionary 2015.\nFrontiers in Psychology 13 (2022), 841534. doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2022.841534\n[4] Ryo Ishii, Fumio Nihei, Yoko Ishii, Atsushi Otsuka, Kazuya Matsuo, Narichika\nNomoto, Atsushi Fukayama, and Takao Nakamura. 2023. Prediction of LoveLike Scores After Speed Dating Based on Pre-obtainable Personal Characteristic\nInformation. In Human-Computer Interaction – INTERACT 2023 (Lecture Notes in\nComputer Science, Vol. 14145), José Abdelnour Nocera, Marta Kristín Lárusdóttir,\nHelen Petrie, Antonio Piccinno, and Marco Winckler (Eds.). Springer Nature\nSwitzerland, Cham, 551–556. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-42293-5_71\n[5] Lawrence I-Kuei Lin. 1989. A Concordance Correlation Coefficient to Evaluate\nReproducibility. Biometrics 45, 1 (1989), 255–268. doi:10.2307/2532051\n[6] Lorenzo Mur-Labadia, Matthew Muckley, Amir Bar, Mido Assran, Koustuv\nSinha, Mike Rabbat, Yann LeCun, Nicolas Ballas, and Adrien Bardes. 2026.\nV-JEPA 2.1: Unlocking Dense Features in Video Self-Supervised Learning.\narXiv:2603.14482 [cs.CV] doi:10.48550/arXiv.2603.14482\n[7] Jianmo Ni, Gustavo Hernandez Abrego, Noah Constant, Ji Ma, Keith Hall, Daniel\nCer, and Yinfei Yang. 2022. Sentence-T5: Scalable Sentence Encoders from Pretrained Text-to-Text Models. In Findings of the Association for Computational\nLinguistics: ACL 2022, Smaranda Muresan, Preslav Nakov, and Aline Villavicencio\n(Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Dublin, Ireland, 1864–1874.\ndoi:10.18653/v1/2022.findings-acl.146\n\n\f", "numLines": 172, "startLine": 2000, "totalLines": 2171}}}
